FXUS61 KCLE 072320

AFDCLE



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Cleveland OH

720 PM EDT Thu Aug 7 2025



.SYNOPSIS...

High pressure centered near the New England coast will continue

to dominate our weather pattern through this weekend. A weak 

cold front will approach the Great Lakes region early to middle 

of next week.



&&



.NEAR TERM /THROUGH FRIDAY NIGHT/...

The main message for the near term forecast period will be more

very nice, summer weather conditions for early to middle of  

August. Our weather pattern is being dominated by high pressure 

that is centered near the New England coast this afternoon. This

high pressure system will remain in place into the weekend and 

continue to bring fair weather conditions to our area. In the

upper levels, there is as ridge of high pressure over the south

central CONUS that will build northeastward into the Ohio 

Valley and lower Great Lakes region through Friday night. 



We are not expecting any pop-up convection today. The mid levels

of the atmosphere is relatively dry and perhaps a small cap to

limit any vertical CU growth. The flat CU from this afternoon 

will fade into the early evening hours. We are expecting a

pleasant evening for any outdoors activities and mostly clear 

skies overnight with low temperatures in the lower to middle

60s. There could be some patchy ground fog that develops in the

favored locations late tonight through sunrise tomorrow 

morning. On Friday, we will see basically a copy of today's 

weather for tomorrow. Mostly sunny to partly cloudy skies with

plenty of sunshine will be around on Friday. We don't really

have any POPs in the forecast but can't rule out a very small 

chance for a pop-up shower down closer to central Ohio tomorrow

afternoon. The POPs are 10 percent or less for that isolated

convection. High temperatures Friday afternoon will be back

into middle to upper 80s. It will remain quiet for Friday night

with overnight low temps ranging from the lower to upper 60s.



&&



.SHORT TERM /SATURDAY THROUGH SUNDAY NIGHT/...

The weekend weather forecast is looking pretty good and hard to

beat as we head into late summer. An elongated upper level ridge

of high pressure will stretch from Texas to the eastern Great 

Lakes region and dominate our weather pattern. At the surface,

a high pressure system will be centered near or just off the 

New England coast this weekend and will continue to influence

our weather this weekend. For both Saturday and Sunday, we will

see mostly sunny to partly cloudy skies with afternoon 

temperatures a few degrees warmer ranging from the middle 80s to

lower 90s. There will be an increase in humidity which will 

make the heat index values up into the middle 90s by Sunday. 

While for the most part, we are expecting dry weather this 

weekend. We can't rule out very isolated pop-up shower or storm 

during the afternoon both days. POPs are 10 percent or less for 

that small chance. Otherwise, it will be quiet weather this 

weekend across our area.



&&



.LONG TERM /MONDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...

We will start off next week hot and humid on Monday with the

upper level ridge of high pressure still over the region.

Afternoon temperatures Monday will be in the upper 80s to lower

90s with heat index values in the middle 90s. A weak cold front

will try to slip down across the Upper Great Lakes by Monday

into into Tuesday but it will run into resistance from the 

ridge of high pressure. Forecast guidance shows this front 

stalling out north of the area by Tuesday or Wednesday of next 

week. We will bring back some chance POPs Tuesday and Wednesday 

with that frontal boundary near by or just north of our area. 

High temperatures for the middle of next week will remain above 

average in the middle 80s to around 90 degrees. POPs may lower 

to slight chance by Thursday as the ridge of high pressure 

pushes back into the area.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z Friday THROUGH Tuesday/...

VFR. Winds southeasterly 5-10kts tonight, becoming southerly 

10kts Friday afternoon with FEW-SCT FL040-050.



Outlook...VFR largely expected through Monday. There is 

potential for some non-VFR visibility every morning in typical 

locations for valley fog.



&&



.MARINE...

Tranquil marine conditions are expected the next several days on 

Lake Erie. High pressure over New England will remain in control 

through the weekend, with generally light south-southwest flow. 

Southerly flow may briefly increase to as high as 15kt over the 

central and eastern basins early Friday and Saturday, though 

otherwise will remain <15kt the next several days. Winds will 

briefly flip onshore due to afternoon lake breezes each afternoon 

through at least Sunday. A cold front is expected to approach Lake 

Erie from the northwest Monday and Tuesday, but not cross until 

Wednesday or Thursday. Southwest flow may increase slightly ahead of 

the front (but should remain under 20kt), with shower/storm chances 

returning ahead of the front as well. Waves may increase briefly to 

2-3 feet Friday and Saturday mornings in the open waters in response 

to briefly elevated southerly winds. Otherwise, waves should remain 

under 2 feet for the next several days across the lake.



&&



.CLE WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

OH...None.

PA...None.

MARINE...None.



&&



$$



SYNOPSIS...77

NEAR TERM...77

SHORT TERM...77

LONG TERM...77

AVIATION...26

MARINE...Sullivan



