[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.8602","issue":"2025-08-07T23:21:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS63","cccc":"KTOP","awipsid":"AFDTOP"}]
=================================================



331

FXUS63 KTOP 072321

AFDTOP



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Topeka KS

621 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Hot and Humid Today/Friday and parts of the area Saturday. A Heat 

Advisory has been issued for all of northeast Kansas Friday with 

heat indices up to 108 degrees possible.



- Storm chances increase across the area Saturday night, with more 

chances Sunday and early Monday. 



- Slightly cooler temperatures build in by Sunday and into next

  week with the increase in cloud cover and rain chances.



&&



.DISCUSSION...

Issued at 201 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Mid-level water vapor imagery this afternoon shows another decaying 

MCS across northern Missouri with ridging over the southwestern US, 

a digging trough over the PNW and weak flow across he southeast. 

With all the precipitation and cloud cover from the Missouri MCS 

staying east of the area, BL mixing and ample solar insolation has 

already pushed temperatures into the low 90s across northeastern 

Kansas. This has led to heat indicies approaching 100 degrees in 

spots. Expect temperatures to top out in the mid to upper 90s this 

afternoon with some hitting triple digits across central and north-

central Kansas. That said, some mid-level clouds have continued to 

develop across central Kansas, likely in response to mid-level 

gravity waves from the Missouri MCS. This may help to keep highs a 

few degrees cooler than previously forecast.



Even warmer conditions are expected Friday as the mid-level ridge 

slides further east. Hot 850mb temps ranging from 25-32C are 

forecast to spread east into the area and with afternoon mixing, 

compressional warming and ample sunshine, low 100 degree high 

temperatures will be expected. Areas across far eastern Kansas may 

only top out in the upper 90s, but 70 degree dewpoints should still 

make things very uncomfortable. Issued a heat advisory for the 

entire area Friday afternoon and early evening to account for heat 

indices reaching upwards of 108 degrees. Make sure to stay hydrated 

and take lots of breaks if planning to be outside.



By Saturday, a digging wave coming out of the PNW will begin to 

eject into and across the northern Rockies. This should deepens a 

surface trough across the Plains, extending from MN to southwest 

Kansas. As the wave continues east, the surface trough will push 

east across Kansas. This should help to provide temporary heat 

relief to areas in northern Kansas as winds shift out of the 

northwest. Areas south of the boundary Saturday afternoon will still 

likely deal with heat as high temperatures climb back into the mid 

to upper 90s. This could again lead to areas seeing heat index 

values approach 105 degrees. Past model runs have sped the passage 

of the surface trough with the boundary bisecting the CWA by 18z or 

so. While this could become a focal point for convection, very warm 

air ahead and aloft will make it very difficult for convection to 

develop. Current thinking is that most cooling aloft will stay off 

to our north and northeast, co-located closer to the main upper wave 

in the northern Plains. Cannot rule out an isolated storm or shower 

to develop during the afternoon/evening Saturday, but not expecting 

anything real widespread at this point. Precipitation chances may 

begin to increase Saturday night into Sunday as the LLJ increases 

and provides lift and overrides the boundary. Given the amount of 

instability, cannot rule out some stronger elevated storms in the 

overnight hours of Saturday/Sunday AM, but confidence is not overly 

high at this time. 



Another shortwave rounding the main trough axis is progged to enter 

Kansas Sunday that could again bring chances for rain and storms in 

the later afternoon/evening. Instability will still be present 

aloft, so will need to watch the possibility of strong storms Sunday 

PM as well. Slightly cooler air filters into the area Monday into 

mid next week as highs top out in the mid to upper 80s. The cooler 

weather seems to mainly be stemmed from an active upper pattern that 

persists through much of next week. With a stalled surface boundary 

hanging around the area, near-daily precipitation chances will be 

expected, albeit low at this time. With possible multiple rounds of 

precipitation, will also need to keep an eye on areal flooding next 

week.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z FRIDAY/...

Issued at 618 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Expect VFR conditions for the next 24 hours. Southerly winds

gusts will diminish through the early evening and overnight but

mixing of the LLJ may cause winds to gust to 20 KTS at time

through the early morning hours of Friday. Southerly winds will

increase after 13Z to 12 to 16 KTS with gusts of 20 to 25 KTS

through the day Friday.



&&



.TOP WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

Heat Advisory from 1 PM to 8 PM CDT Friday for KSZ008-KSZ009-

KSZ010-KSZ011-KSZ012-KSZ020-KSZ021-KSZ022-KSZ023-KSZ024-KSZ026-

KSZ034-KSZ035-KSZ036-KSZ037-KSZ038-KSZ039-KSZ040-KSZ054-KSZ055-

KSZ056-KSZ058-KSZ059.



&&



$$



DISCUSSION...Griesemer

AVIATION...Gargan



