=================================================



410

FXUS64 KLCH 072325

AFDLCH



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Lake Charles LA

625 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



...New AVIATION...



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Expect a continuation of typical summertime conditions through 

  the end of the week with higher rain chances in lower Acadiana.

 

- Rain chances increase a bit areawide over the weekend and into 

  early next week as a disturbance moves across the northern Gulf 

  Coast.



&&



.SHORT TERM...

(Tonight through Saturday night)

Issued at 1226 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Mid-morning surface analysis shows weak high pressure extending from 

New England across the Appalachians and into the NW Gulf, while what 

remains of a stalled front is draped along the southeastern coast 

from the Carolinas to Alabama. Aloft, broad ridging is situated from 

southern California to the southern Plains, with the forecast area 

on the eastern periphery of the ridge. While moisture has returned 

at the surface dry air aloft provided by the ridge should help to 

keep convection in check today. CAMs guidance shows very isolated 

showers and thunderstorms developing through the afternoon, similar 

to the last few days. Temperature wise, we should again top out in 

the low to mid 90s this afternoon amid mostly sunny skies.



Tomorrow, ridging aloft begins to gradually weaken and retreat 

westward as a weakness develops over the southeastern US/northeastern 

Gulf region.  While this weakness slides westward Friday into 

Saturday high pressure at the surface will continue to dominate, 

which will help to keep rain chances somewhat in check. Both 

tomorrow and Saturday the highest rain chances will be across the 

southern half of the area, with minimal precip expected further 

inland. However, even these "highest rain chances" are only in the 

40-60% range. 



&&



.LONG TERM...

(Sunday through next Wednesday)

Issued at 1226 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



By Sunday surface high pressure extending into the area from the NE 

begins to retreat a bit, allowing moisture to increase a bit 

further, while the weakness aloft gradually slides into southeastern 

Texas. This set up will bring slightly higher rain chances to the 

forecast area for the end of the weekend, with 20-40 POPs generally 

north of the I-190 corridor and 50-70 POPs elsewhere. 



Moving into the work week, there is some model differences but the 

general consensus now seems to be that the upper level weakness will 

move into interior TX and become absorbed by a weak trough dipping 

across the Plains. There also seems to be some indication that some 

sort of surface trough will develop along the north-central Gulf 

Coast and bring a surge of moisture into the Gulf Coast states. 

While the highest moisture looks to remain more to our east, 

definitely think we could see a decent uptick in PWATs Mon through 

Wed. This set up will bring increased rain chances each day through 

the beginning of the work week, especially through the afternoon 

hours/peak heating. 



&&



.AVIATION...

(00Z TAFS)

Issued at 619 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Mainly VFR conditions this evening with lower CIGs in the 

vicinity of the lingering showers and storms mainly along and

south of the I-10 corridor. Carried VCTS for the rest of the 

evening as a result of the convection, with tapering expected over

the next few hours. Similar to yesterday, we are seeing the same

signal for patchy fog tonight, however this was only added to KAEX

at the time due to the low confidence at other sites. Tomorrow 

afternoon we will see another round of diurnal activity, mainly 

across the Acadiana area and coastal terminals. 



&&



.MARINE...

Issued at 1226 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



A light onshore flow with low seas will continue through end of

the week as weak high pressure nudges into the area from the 

east. A few nocturnal showers and storms will be possible each 

morning. Over the weekend a weakness aloft will move east to west

across the Gulf. Winds will increase just slightly in response

while remaining east to southeasterly, while coverage of showers 

and storms will become more scattered to widespread.



&&



.FIRE WEATHER...

Issued at 1226 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



MinRH values are forecast to be in the 45 to 55 percent range 

until we see a slight uptick in moisture at the end of this 

weekend. From there the range will be in the 50 to 70 percent 

range. Rain chances will be higher along and south of the I-10 

corridor each day, especially over the Acadiana region. Rain

chances increase a bit area wide Sunday into early next week.



&&



