415

FXUS63 KOAX 072325

AFDOAX



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Omaha/Valley NE

625 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Heat remains in place through Friday with highs climbing into

  the 90s. Heat index values tomorrow will range from 102-110.



- Strong to severe thunderstorms may be possible Friday and

  Saturday.



- A cold front will bring cooler temperatures to the region, and

  additional chances for showers and storms.



&&



.DISCUSSION...

Issued at 345 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Visible satellite analysis this afternoon reveals an upper low

spinning over the PACNW, a cold front draped over western South

Dakota, and a shortwave pushing southeast out of central Iowa.

There's widespread cloud cover at 2500 feet corresponding to

some significant moisture transport and some low-level

divergence just below the EML. This has lead to cooler high

temperatures than anticipated today. Highs are struggling to 

hit 90F. Dewpoints faced no such hurdle as every station in the 

CWA has a 72F+ Td. It's sticky out there. 



A very weak shortwave at H7 will bring an uptick in cloud cover

overnight but very little else. South-southeasterly winds remain

relatively robust at 10-15 knots overnight, helping temps to

hold on to 70s through the diurnal cycle. 'Tis the season for

unexpected overnight convection, but with the LLJ pointed more

toward the St. Joseph, MO area, any surprise lightning and

thunder may be focused too far to the south and east to affect

too many Nebraskans or Iowans who do their back to school

shopping in Omaha. 



.FRIDAY...



Sunny skies and quick southerly winds should drive temps into

the 90s tomorrow with the Fairbury area / south-central Nebraska

making a run at 100F. Leftover convective boundaries / cloud-

cover from possible convection tonight could play spoiler, but

chances of that look less than typical for August. With

dewpoints remaining in the 70s, heat indices will push above

100F and should peak at 105 or more for most locations. For this

reason, a HEAT ADVISORY has been issued for the entire forecast

area from 1pm to 8pm. It's bounded by heat advisories from other

offices. 



Aforementioned cutoff low at H5 traversing our northern 

international border drags a powerful cold front through the 

Northern Plains, driving into eastern Nebraska around sunset 

Friday night. 



A The SPC has reduced the threat of severe weather for this 

area on Friday evening as CAM guidance and has backed off PoPs 

(more in line with forecaster thinking at 25-35%). MUCape 

pushes over 5000 J/kg at times, but shear is lacking and the 

best forcing for ascent is well to the north as the front slows 

and eventually stalls in eastern Nebraska. Have maintained PoPs 

of 15-30% for areas north of I-30 (Blair to Columbus) before 

sunrise Saturday morning. 



.SATURDAY...



Highs on Saturday will suffer with the shallow cooler air in

place plus clouds and possible showers/storms associated with

the stalled front. Most will peak in the 80s, but a 90 is

possible for those closer to Missouri. 



Best chances of those showers and storms (50-70% south of I-80) on

Saturday will wait for the low- level nocturnal jet. PWAT 

values approach 2" and moisture advection pushes directly over 

the stalled front, leading to flash flooding concerns overnight.

Model guidance suggests 2-4" could fall at a rate of 1" per 

hour. The soil is already soggy.



.THE REST...



The front is progged to stubbornly move very little for Sunday,

providing another opportunity for showers and storms (50-70%)

for the southern half of the CWA, especially after dark. Highs

will be in the lower 80s for Sunday and Monday with general

trofing over the central CONUS. As the H5 pattern becomes more 

zonal mid-week, temps improve by a few degrees Fahrenheit. 

These numbers are close to seasonal norms for mid- August.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z FRIDAY/...

Issued at 625 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



VFR conditions favored through the period with any lingering lower

clouds expected to dissipate early in the period. A few pieces

of guidance hint at fog development early Friday in northeast

Nebraska, but think the combination of passing mid to high

clouds and stronger winds aloft should keep any development

pretty patchy. Winds aloft will also be strong enough for some

low level wind shear early Friday, especially at LNK with 40 

kts out of the southwest at 2000 ft agl. Otherwise, expect

southeasterly to southerly surface winds, with gusts of 20-25 

kts starting mid to late Friday morning.





&&



.OAX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

NE...Heat Advisory from 1 PM to 8 PM CDT Friday for NEZ011-012-

     015>018-030>034-042>045-050>053-065>068-078-088>093.

IA...Heat Advisory from 1 PM to 8 PM CDT Friday for IAZ043-055-056-

     069-079-080-090-091.



&&



$$



DISCUSSION...Nicolaisen

AVIATION...CA



