417

FXUS64 KLIX 072325

AFDLIX



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service New Orleans LA

625 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



...New AVIATION...



.SHORT TERM...

(This evening through Friday night)

Issued at 106 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



There is less thunderstorm activity than yesterday, and it is mostly

along the coast. We still have upper level areas of high pressure

around us with a relative low over us. Yesterday's weak front has

drifted just offshore drawing dryer air into the area. This is a 

very typical summertime setup and the low pressure...really just

pressure not quite as high as that surrounding us...is allowing

for upward movement of the air, but the drier air doesn't support

rainfall generation. We can expect the same pattern at least

through the next 48 hours. Rain chances greater near the coast,

high temps in the low 90s and feels like temps just over 100, and

light, variable winds. While we don't see any chance for severe

storms, we could see some strong thunderstorms generating

lightning, high rain rates, and strong winds in the vicinity of 

the storm. This could lead to localized flooding and isolated wind

damage.



&&



.LONG TERM...

(Saturday through Wednesday night)

Issued at 106 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



By Saturday, we'll see the upper trough over the SE CONUS pinch off 

and retrograde southwestward into the northern Gulf as a Rex 

Blocking Pattern sets up as ridging builds over the Great Lakes and 

troughing gets stuck underneath. Falling heights in combination with 

a weak inverted surface trough moving in from the east will 

gradually increase afternoon convective coverage, especially on 

Sunday and Monday, as the upper trough backs to the west and the 

inverted surface trough moves across the area. This is still a

situation where the differences between highest and lowest

pressure are subtle. Confidence is currently highest in higher 

PoPs for Sunday, but these subtle features are more difficult to 

get the timing right. PoPs could remain elevated above 

climatological norms through Tuesday based on latest guidance. By 

the middle of next week, the nose of the Bermuda ridging may 

encroach from the east damping down PoPs and allowing temps to 

nudge upward to above average.



&&



.AVIATION...

(00Z TAFS)

Issued at 622 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



TAFs are generally VFR this forecast cycle, outside of any 

terminals getting directly impacted by showers and storms. If that

happens, that could bring both ceilings and visibilities down to 

MVFR to IFR. Due to the current ongoing convection, tried to time 

out tempo groups for the terminals most likely to see storms in

the next 1 to 3 hours (BTR, NEW, MSY). Otherwise, had to stick to

PROB30s for tomorrow's afternoon storm chances. -BL



&&



.MARINE...

Issued at 106 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



With diurnal convective pattern combined with areas of slightly

lower pressure offshore, expect nighttime and daytime convection

somewhere in the marine areas. Overall wind velocities will be

light and variable but speeds will increase in the vicinity of any

storms that do occur. Any microscale boundaries that form could 

be the source of waterspout formation and these will also cause

variability in wind velocities.



&&



