=================================================



601

FXUS66 KHNX 072328

AFDHNX



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Hanford CA

430 PM PDT Thu Aug 7 2025





.KEY MESSAGES...

1. A warming trend is underway as triple digit heat will be seen

through early next week.



2. There is a 15 to 20 percent chance of high temperatures of at

least 103 degrees Today before increasing to 40 to 60 percent 

chance during the weekend. 



3. Minor fire risk with widespread minimum relative humidity 

between 15 and 25 percent for the non-desert areas. Minimum

relative humidity expected to be less than 10 percent in the 

Mojave Desert with breezy winds in the afternoon to evening.



&&



.DISCUSSION...

High Pressure Ridge finally taking shape over the West as 

Central California see’s a return of Triple digits today. While 

a disturbance moving through the Pacific Northwest and Extreme 

Northern California, the ridge over the Southwest will now 

dictate temperature conditions over the area. However, the 

slight southward shift of the center of the Four-Corners High 

will keep a westerly wind component over the area and limiting 

the amount of instability and moisture entering for several 

days. Will see little change on the pattern as ensemble 

Probability of Exceedance (PoE) of reaching 100 degree at high 

percentage levels through early week.



Ensemble PoE has been very consistent with the onset of 

widespread triple digits across the San Joaquin Valley this 

Thursday. So far, 24 hour change in temperatures continue to run

2 to 4 degree warmer and maintaining the warming trend. At this

point, PoE of reaching 100 degrees is reaching into the 90% 

range until next Tuesday. Ensemble temperature analysis does 

signal toward possible cooling next Wednesday and Thursday as 

PoE of 100 degrees falls mostly below 50%. At the same time, PoE

of reaching 105 degrees never goes above 50% outside of the 

Interstate-5 corridor. Therefore, will keep the range of Max 

temperatures between 99 and 104 degrees during the period.



Long range ensemble analysis still showing minimal deviation of

where it is positioning the center of the four-corners high. The

placement of the center about south to southwest of the Four

Corners will play a big factor in the transport of moisture into

the region. Ensemble winds aloft maintain a southwesterly

direction that will in turn will keep higher precip-water values

east of Central California. With a lack of Monsoonal moisture

pushing into Central California, convection will be inhibited

across the Sierra Nevada as the region remains dry. While

ensemble wind prognosis show only light breezy conditions

developing each afternoon, with some fire weather concerns

existing due to the nature of how dry (especially Kern County)

conditions are across the West. Even with a disturbance moving

through the area next week, the district will remain dry.



&&



.AVIATION...

00Z Update: 



VFR conditions are expected in the San Joaquin Valley for the next 

24 hours.



&&



.AIR QUALITY ISSUES...

None.



&&



.CERTAINTY...



The level of certainty for days 1 and 2 is high.

The level of certainty for days 3 through 7 is medium.



Certainty levels include low...medium...and high. Please visit

www.weather.gov/hnx/certainty.html for additional information

and/or to provide feedback.



&&



.HNX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

None.



&&



