AFDLZK



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Little Rock AR

629 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



...New AVIATION...



.DISCUSSION...

Issued at 210 AM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Skies were mostly clear and temperatures were in the 70s across AR 

early this Thursday morning. A gradual warming trend will 

continue today, lasting through at least the weekend. 



Aloft, an upper ridge was situated between the Four Corners and Srn 

Plains. Locally, NW flow will be in place through at least Friday. 

Beginning Sunday, an upper level trough will move into the Nrn CONUS 

with the base of the trough slowly pivoting across the Srn/Cntrl 

Plains. Across the MS Valley, the region will reside between 

suppressed ridging to the W and E, and the trough just to the N and 

W, through early to middle of next week. 



The main change with this forecast update is increased PoP chances 

from Monday through Wednesday. Diurnally driven showers and 

thunderstorms are anticipated to develop. This activity should 

decrease in coverage after sunset each evening with the loss of 

daytime heating. 



Low level return flow will increase through the weekend, becoming 

S/SWrly, in response to a deepening low across the Cntrl Plains. 

Increasing Srly flow will ramp up dew points as well as 

atmospheric precipitable water values. PW values increasing 

to/above 2 inches is probable as early as Monday which will serve 

as a contributor for increased afternoon convection.



Today, highs will climb into the lower to upper 90s. Heat index 

values will approach or exceed 100 degrees across a large portion 

of AR starting today and continue through the period. A Heat 

Advisory will go into effect today across Wrn portions of the AR 

River Valley where heat indices exceed 105 degrees. For Friday, 

Heat Advisories have been expanded Ewrd to cover all of the AR 

River Valley. Additional heat headlines may be needed beyond 

Friday but those will be analyzed on a day or two in advance 

basis. Aforementioned showers and thunderstorms, in addition to 

increase cloud coverage, could provide some relief from the heat 

early next week. 



&&



.AVIATION...

(00Z TAFS)

Issued at 628 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Widespread VFR conditions are expected through the period. Winds

will be mostly light and variable overnight. The becoming out of

the south and southeast by later Friday morning.



&&



