[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.8878","issue":"2025-08-07T23:29:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS64","cccc":"KMOB","awipsid":"AFDMOB"}]
=================================================



608

FXUS64 KMOB 072329

AFDMOB



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Mobile AL

629 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



...New Aviation...



.DISCUSSION...

Issued at 145 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



The axis of the upper trough over the eastern CONUS will gradually

weaken as it shifts to the Eastern Seaboard by Friday afternoon. A

weak upper ridge will then setup over the northeastern CONUS over

the weekend, with an inverted upper trough forming over the 

western Gulf and lower Mississippi River region. This trough, 

however, will get pushed further west through early next week as a

second upper high pressure ridge builds over our region from the 

east. Light and variable surface winds will continue through

tonight, followed by light easterly winds Friday into early next 

week as a surface ridge sets up across the southeast states.



Friday will likely be one of the driest days throughout the

forecast package as the first upper trough slips east of our area,

with mainly afternoon isolated showers and thunderstorms expected

south of Highway 84. A typical diurnal pattern will continue, with

scattered to at times numerous daily showers and thunderstorms

returning for Saturday through the middle of next week. Pulse 

thunderstorms are possible each afternoon given ample instability 

and minimal shear, so we can't rule out a few strong storms with 

gusty winds. Frequent lightning and heavy bouts of rain are likely

as well.



Temperatures remain steady over the next seven days, with highs in

the upper 80s to lower 90s. With surface dewpoints ranging from 70

to 75 degrees, afternoon apparent temperatures (heat indices) are

expected to be in upper 90s. Lows should be in the lower to middle

70s interior areas, and upper 70s along the coast.



Beach Forecast - The rip current risk will be LOW tonight through

the weekend. /22



&&



.AVIATION...

(00Z TAFS)

Issued at 620 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Scattered showers and thunderstorms across the forecast area at

23z are bringing local drops in conditions to mid/low level MVFR.

This convection will decrease through the evening, then redevelop

offshore late tonight. Isolated to scattered shower and 

thunderstorms are expected late Friday morning into the afternoon,

bringing local drops in conditions to mid/low level MVFR. 

Coverage is expected to be the greatest closer to the coast. 

/16



&&



.MARINE...

Issued at 145 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Winds and seas will be higher near storms through early next week.

Reductions in visibility are likely in heavy rains and frequent 

lightning will also pose a hazard to recreational and commercial 

boating interests. Otherwise, expect light and variable winds to

continue through Thursday, becoming a light easterly flow Friday 

into early next week as a surface ridge sets up across the 

southeast states. /22



&&



