FXUS63 KLOT 072330

AFDLOT



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Chicago/Romeoville, IL

630 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Seasonably hot and humid weather through the weekend.



- Though majority of the time will be dry, the chances for

  occasional showers and thunderstorms will increase late in the

  weekend into at least the first half of next week.



&&



.UPDATE...

Issued at 614 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



An early update to the forecast has been made to effectively

limit the threat of thunderstorms to our area to 20% (the 

minimum threshold to be formally advertised in our gridded 

database). 



Recent observational data depict a subtle zone of low-level

confluence extending from Taylorville to Decatur and Lexington,

IL, along the northeastern flank of a sprawling MCV drifting 

southeastward toward St. Louis, MO. Low-level confluence 

appears to weaken with northward extent (further away from the 

MCV), though recent satellite imagery does depict a pencil-thin 

line of cumulus clouds extending as far north as Seneca, IL. In 

addition, radar imagery depicts a similar-thin line of weak 

radar returns extending as far north as DeKalb, IL. 



Near and east of the zone of low-level confluence, temperatures

in the mid 80s, dew points in the mid 70s, and mid-level lapse 

rates around 7 K/km are supporting MLCAPE of 2500 to locally 

3500 J/kg. In spite of little to no deep-layer shear (RAP- 

analyzed wind fields show less than 20kt of flow up to 20kft), 

such high MLCAPE will continue to support quickily-developing 

thunderstorms that similarly quickly collapse with a threat for

localized microbursts within the low-level confluence zone. The

northward extent of this threat is unclear (owing to the 

aforementioned weakening confluence north of Lexington, IL). 

However, can envision a few storms developing across parts of 

Livingston, Grundy, and La Salle counties over the next few 

hours particularly if the developing storms to our south can 

burp out a northward surge of outflow. At this point, am not 

seeing any signs of this happening, suggesting that all areas

will stay dry this evening. For now, will carry a 20% chance of

storms across our southwest just to be safe. 



Turning attention to the overnight hours, the threat for

additional thunderstorms north of I-80 appears to be dwindling.

What once looked like a concerning scenario for heavy-rain

producing thunderstorms 24 hours ago appeared to be contingent 

on an expansive reservoir or instability present across our 

region this evening ahead of an MCV moving into southwestern 

Wisconsin. With the earlier MCS across Iowa effectively leaving 

a giant hole in instability and moisture near the Mississippi 

River (that is lifting northeastward into northern Illinois), 

and no apparent MCV in southwestern Wisconsin (as convection 

this morning evolved completely differently than most CAMs 

advertised), neither the previously-identified trigger nor 

reservoir of instability needed for an overnight round of storms

appear to be present. Speaking of CAMS, the only ones that 

actually did alright and showed the earlier MCS in Iowa were the

NSSL MPAS-core WRFs that initialized at 12Z yesterday morning. 

Those same model runs show nothing in our area tonight, matching

observational trends.



Now, there is a subtle upper-level wave moving southeastward

along the Iowa and Minnesota border region (as mentioned in the

Short term Discussion below). As this feature moves toward our 

area overnight, would expect at least a modest low-level jet to 

develop and nose into northern IL, provided it is not disrupted

by convection closer to St. Louis. So, will still watch for any

signs of blossoming ACCAS across eastern Iowa tonight in case 

something were to manage to develop within the localized minima 

in instability. Similarly, will maintain 20% PoPs along and 

north of I-80 out of an abundance of caution. With that said, 

strongly suspect all areas will remain dry overnight.



Updated products will be sent soon. 



Borchardt



&&



.DISCUSSION...

Issued at 245 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Through Friday Night:



Convection associated MCS that lasted much of the morning 

across southern IA and northern MO has finally begun to fade.

Remnant MCV over extreme northeast MO is pushing southeastward

and will track well southwest of our CWA. Visible satellite

imagery shows evidence of subsidence and much more stable 

conditions in the wake of this MCV over much of IA and 

northwest IL. In general, guidance has done a poor job of

handling this MCS/MCV, which does lead to decrease confidence in

tonight's forecast across the Midwest.



Gravity waves can be seen emanating from this MCS with pattern

of enhancement and suppression of cumulus field noted over

Wisconsin and northeast Illinois as gravity waves passes. Other

than those transient gravity waves and some enhanced 

convergence along the lake breeze over northeast Illinois, there

isn't much else to force/focus convection this afternoon. The

threat of isolated convection should continue over northeast

half or so of our CWA, with the highest chances over northeast

IL. 



Guidance is really all over the place with the threat of

convection in our area tonight. Water vapor imagery does show a

well defined, compact vort max tracking eastward across far 

northern IA. Air mass immediately ahead of this vort is pretty 

strongly capped, so while there has been some blossoming of 

stratocumulus field over north central IA, thus far, haven't 

seen any precip. As this vort continues east, the associated 

ascent is likely to reach an area of weaker inhibition and 

moderate to strong instability over northeast IA/SW WI later 

this afternoon. This could result in some convection developing 

and tracking eastward across southern WI this evening. 



Low confidence in how widespread convection will become with

this feature and how well it will be sustained into the late

evening and overnight. The northern MO MCS effectively

overturned the steeper mid level lapse rates, with these weaker

mid level lapse rates expected over northern IL and southern WI

tonight. While there will be some modest low level warm air

advection with 15-20kt southerly flow off the deck, the nose of

the actual low level jet is progged to remain across the 

eastern Great Plains and lower Missouri River Valley region. 

While this would suggest convection should struggle to last into

the night, the vort max looks fairly strong on water vapor 

imagery, so it could overperform with convection. Model guidance

was of little use for the forecast tonight, so based the POP 

forecast largely on observational trends.



Highest chances of showers and storms tonight look to be across

southern WI into far northern IL where we've maintained chance

PoPs (30-40%). Air mass will be sufficiently moist to support

thunderstorms with heavy rain, but would really like to see

stronger low level flow and corresponding theta-e advection to

be really concerned with locally heavy rainfall threat. Given

how poorly resolved features are in model guidance, the oncoming

evening shift will need to closely monitor observational trends

and better refine the thunderstorm threat for tonight.



Barring any surprise MCV magic, Friday looks to be largely dry

with partly to mostly sunny skies, hot and humid conditions.

Dewpoints have been overperforming a bit today, this could be

partially due to subsident inversion associated with NE MO MCV

and cirrus limiting mixing a bit. Tomorrow, mixing could be a

bit more efficient, but given recent trends have trended

dewpoints tomorrow toward the higher end of the guidance

spectrum. Peak heat indices Friday afternoon are not expected to

reach heat advisory criteria, topping out well into the 90s to 

around 100.



- Izzi



Saturday through Thursday:



Saturday will be another hot and very humid day, possibly with

heat indices a tick or two higher than Friday. Forecast 925 mb 

temps came down a tad vs. previous cycles, so any mid 90s highs 

will likely be on a highly localized basis in the heart of the 

Chicago urban heat island. Otherwise highs near/around 90F to the 

lower 90s appear on track. Dew points into the 70s, highest over 

farming areas due to evapotranspiration should translate to peak 

heat indices near to around 100F. If temperatures pan out close to

as expected and dew points overperform in spots, some locations 

could see heat indices up in the 103-105F range. The current 

expectation is for heat advisory issuance for any of our counties 

to be unlikely, though can't completely rule it out. 



Despite the presence of strong ridging aloft (500 mb heights of

~590-592 DaM locally), later Saturday into the evening and 

overnight hours, capping may be sufficiently eroded for some 

convective potential towards and after sunset. Our area won't be 

too far removed from shortwaves rounding seasonably deep 

troughing over southern Manitoba, along with the 500 mb ridge axis

being centered over the central and eastern Great Lakes. As such,

isolated to widely scattered gusty pulse type thunderstorms 

(given steep low-mid level lapse rates and plentiful DCAPE) are 

plausible from the late afternoon through the evening hours in the

northwest 1/3 or so of the CWA. The large scale pattern doesn't 

appear particularly favorable for the maintenance of an intense 

MCS overnight, though this summer has had a preponderance of 

meandering MCVs that could augment convective evolution on the 

mesoscale. The best chance of continued thunderstorm activity 

overnight Saturday night into Sunday morning looks to be for areas

near/northwest of I-55.



While lingering convection (if any) into Sunday morning would play

a role in temperature trends, temperatures should still reach 

into the upper 80s to lower 90s for the southeast 2/3 or so of the

CWA (a bit cooler northwest). Similar dew points to those of

Saturday into the 70s will yield peak heat indices over the 

warmest spots in the mid-upper 90s (lower 90s northwest CWA).

There's not much large scale forcing for ascent evident Sunday

afternoon, though would anticipate capping eroding more quickly,

which could set the stage for isolated to widely scattered pulsey

"airmass type" thunderstorms, particularly near/north of I-80 in

Illinois.



Sunday night through mid-late next work week, the eastern CONUS

mid-upper level ridging is forecast to break down/flatten out, 

which would entail temps not quite as warm as this weekend, but a 

high dew point/high humidity air mass persisting. The flattening 

out of the ridge would also place the local area in a more 

favorable position to see multiple rounds of convection as 

numerous impulses overtop the ridge. For this reason, PoPs

generally in the 20-50% range for the Sunday night through Tuesday

period appear reasonable. In a change from previous cycles on 

some of the ensemble guidance, the potential for a break in the 

humidity and regular shower/t-storm chances in the mid-late week 

period may have diminished. 



Deep layer shear should remain marginal throughout this upcoming 

stretch, in turn limiting the chance for widespread organized 

strong to severe convection (but certainly not eliminating any 

threat for localized severe weather). The prolonged high PWATs and

slow storm motions from weak flow aloft will result in at least 

localized threats for flash flooding. 



Castro



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 06Z SATURDAY/...

Issued at 630 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Only forecast concern is the winds through the period. Southerly

winds will diminish under 10kts with sunset this evening and may

become south/southeast for a short time, then becoming southerly

or south/southwest overnight. Southerly winds will increase into

the 10-12kt range by mid/late morning with gusts to 20kt

possible through late afternoon Friday. Speeds/gusts will

diminish with sunset Friday evening with directions possibly

turning south/southeast.



Thunderstorm chances have decreased considerably in recent hours

with this evening expected to be dry for the terminals. There

remains a low chance (10%) for a few showers or thunderstorms

toward daybreak but confidence is too low for any mention with

this forecast. cms



