644

FXUS63 KAPX 072331

AFDAPX



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Gaylord MI

731 PM EDT Thu Aug 7 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Increasingly warm and humid conditions into this weekend.



- Periodic shower and storm chances expected through this weekend, 

  but nothing widespread or too significant anticipated.



&&



.SHORT TERM /THROUGH FRIDAY/...

Issued at 623 PM EDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Pattern Synopsis/Forecast:



Classic long-wave configuration to get some true heat and humidity 

into the Northwoods...with east expanding and building mid/upper 

level ridge across the Great Lakes well ahead of digging shortwave 

trough into Montana. Maturing sub H7 layer south to southwest flow 

the result, with an increasing tap into this layer of sub-tropical 

heat and humidity from the Gulf of America and the northern Gulf 

States. That flow will only steadily increase with time as we head 

through the end of this week...made to do so by continued slow east 

progression of upper level features noted above.



Primary Forecast Concerns:



Shower/storm potential and temperature trends.



Details:



Still looking at the potential for a few showers and thunderstorms 

to percolate this afternoon, especially across northeast lower 

Michigan where low level convergence is maximized (partially lake 

breeze driven) within axis of 1000 J/kg of solar induced mixed layer 

cape. Definitely not expecting anything too widespread, with most 

areas no doubt remaining dry into this evening. May see a few 

additional showers and storms form overnight as nose of weak low 

level jet to our southwest pushes plume of elevated instability into 

the region. Forcing is weak at best and of course solar support will 

be completely lost. However, northward expanding instability 

gradient within plume of greater than 1.50 inch precipitable water 

values at least supports shower/storm mention. Again, definitely not 

expecting anything too widespread or significant...with much better 

odds most of the area remains dry right into the morning hours of 

Friday. Definitely a mild late summer night, with lows ranging 

through the 60s.



Persistent moisture advection and another round of increasing 

diurnally-driven instability again argues for at least the threat 

for a few additional shower and storms through the day Friday. While 

weak and disorganized wind fields should preclude any severe 

concern, given the plentiful moisture through the vertical does 

support at least brief periods of heavy rain with any more organized 

convection. Gotta state the obvious again, most areas will likely 

remain dry.



Definitely a warm one, with widespread 80s for highs (local 90?). 

Add dewpoints well up into the 60s, and we are looking at heat 

indices a few degrees warmer yet.



&&



.LONG TERM /FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...

Issued at 623 PM EDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Pattern Synopsis/Forecast:



Deep southwest flow in amplified upper level environment will 

continue to pump heat and humidity into the region through Sunday, 

and perhaps Monday as well. Flow regime looks to become a touch more 

zonal into mid-week, with at least some support within the long 

range ensemble suite that flow will begin to go through more 

amplification as we head into the end of the week. 



Primary Forecast Concerns:



Temperature trends and shower/storm concerns through the period.



Details:



Heat looks to reach its pinnacle on Saturday when low level thermal 

progs support highs near or a bit over 90 degrees. Humidity values 

remain elevated as well, supporting heat indices a few degrees 

warmer. Currently not anticipating heat headline criteria, but 

uncomfortably warm for sure. May see a few diurnally-driven 

showers/storms with all that heat as well, although a bit of drying 

through the lower levels argues otherwise. Current trends support 

main arrival of deep layer forcing support via approaching troughing 

and attendant slow moving cold front to arrive sometime later 

Saturday night through Monday. Simple pattern recognition supports 

the threat for at least a few showers and storms through this 

period, although timing of actual "best" potential remains 

problematic. Wind fields look to remain a bit disorganized, so 

currently not anticipating any significant severe concerns. Rain 

might be a bit more problematic with deep moisture and some support 

for at least a few rounds of more organized convection. Of course, 

this being northern Michigan, odds of any real impactful rain 

amounts remain low indeed.



Post-frontal environment is not an overly cool one, supporting highs 

Tuesday and beyond running near to a bit above normal. Questions 

abound about additional rain chances through the week, so a simple 

consensus guidance blend approach is best utilized for now. 

Unfortunately, and is often the case, this forces a multi-period low 

end shower/storm mention, when in all reality, any actual rain 

threats will be relegated to much shorter time windows.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z SATURDAY/...

Issued at 731 PM EDT Thu Aug 7 2025



TSRA near APN will diminish heading thru the evening. Will be

keeping an eye on the CIU area for a possible TSRA later

tonight. Otherwise just a slight chance for a shower/storm 

tonight into Friday. Will be watching for potential fog tonight,

though some semblance of a breeze and partial cloud cover should

mitigate this to a degree. Light southerly winds.



&&



.APX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

MI...None.

MARINE...None.



&&



$$



SHORT TERM...MSB

LONG TERM...MSB

AVIATION...JZ



