=================================================



673

FXUS64 KAMA 072333

AFDAMA



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Amarillo TX

633 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



...New AVIATION...



.KEY MESSAGES...

Issued at 120 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



- Heat Advisories have been issued for today and Friday across

  portions of the Panhandles due to widespread temperatures in the

  triple digits.



- Thunderstorms will be possible each day over the next week and

  temperatures will drop down to near average starting this

  weekend.



&&



.SHORT TERM...

(This evening through Friday night)

Issued at 120 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



According to the latest RAP H500 analysis, broad high pressure

remains in place with the center of the high across central New

Mexico. H850 temperatures have warmed up to 30 Celsius based on

the 18z RAOB from KAMA and the temperatures should continue to

warm up a few more degrees this afternoon. These low level

temperatures will lead to widespread highs of 100 degrees or

slightly warmer for the Panhandles this afternoon. The ongoing

Heat Advisory will remain in effect through early this evening.



With the upper level high pressure further to the west, this

should allow a very weak shortwave trough to move over portions of

the northern Panhandles this evening. Convection has begun across

southeastern Colorado and there are additional cumulus developing

over northeastern New Mexico into the far northwestern Panhandles

at the time of this writing. Even though CAMs have mostly backed

off on the potential for storms this afternoon, still am keeping

at least 10-15 percent chances for isolated showers or storms for

this evening. With dry air at the surface, virga is more likely

than showers or storms and with DCAPE values upwards of 2000 to

2200 J/kg, isolated damaging wind gusts will be possible.



Very minimal changes are expected on Friday with another hot day

for the Panhandles featuring widespread triple digit heat. A Heat

Advisory has already been issued for the central and eastern

Panhandles due to the potential for the very hot temperatures.

This area is indicated well in the heat risk category with

widespread major impacts for the Panhandles for tomorrow. Another

shortwave rounding the ridge may kick off more showers or storms

tomorrow afternoon and early evening. It will once again be dry at

the surface tomorrow so much of the showers/storms will be virga,

but some rain still may reach the ground. Strong to locally

damaging wind gusts will once again be possible with any showers

or storms that form or move over the CWA.



Muscha



&&



.LONG TERM...

(Saturday through next Wednesday)

Issued at 120 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Daily thunderstorm chances and near average temperatures are the

key points of the long term forecast. High pressure aloft is

forecast to retrograde back to the west this weekend as troughing

digs south over the Rockies. A surface cold front is forecast to

move over the region on Saturday which will aid in starting the

cool down for the Panhandles. This feature may also lead to

thunderstorm chances this weekend. Model guidance does not agree

on timing and location of the front during this time frame. That

potential will need to be monitored as the front could be a focal

point for a heavy rain threat this weekend. With moisture

increasing across the southern High Plains, strong to severe

thunderstorms will be possible each of the days as well. Details

regarding any severe storm threat are unknown at this point but

the potential is there. High pressure may start to move back

towards the east late next week which could lead to temperatures

warming back up above average to end next work week.



Muscha



&&



.AVIATION...

(00Z TAFS)

Issued at 625 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



As of early this evening, showers and thunderstorms were still

present across the western Oklahoma Panhandle and slowly moving 

east. However, these storms are struggling to maintain themselves 

and are currently unlikely to impact KGUY. Meanwhile, both KAMA 

and KDHT are both VFR and expected to stay that way through the 

overnight, albeit with some surface wind gust present. The only 

thing that may need to be watch is some slight haze from ongoing 

prescribed burns occurring one county over to the east of KAMA. 

However, wind direction should keep the smoke out of the area for

the most part.





&&



.AMA WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

TX...Heat Advisory until 8 PM CDT this evening for TXZ001>015-017>020-

     317.



     Heat Advisory from 1 PM to 8 PM CDT Friday for TXZ003>005-

     008>010-013>015-018>020-317.



OK...Heat Advisory until 8 PM CDT this evening for OKZ001>003.



     Heat Advisory from 1 PM to 8 PM CDT Friday for OKZ002-003.



&&



$$



SHORT TERM...05

LONG TERM....05

AVIATION...11



