[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.8946","issue":"2025-08-07T23:33:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS61","cccc":"KBGM","awipsid":"AFDBGM"}]
=================================================



677

FXUS61 KBGM 072333

AFDBGM



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Binghamton NY

733 PM EDT Thu Aug 7 2025



.SYNOPSIS...

Mostly sunny but hazy conditions continue with another stray 

thunderstorm or two possible over the Catskills into this

evening. High pressure shifts slightly east of the region this 

weekend resulting in a slow increase in the heat and humidity 

by early next week.



&&



.NEAR TERM /THROUGH FRIDAY NIGHT/...

135 PM Update



High pressure will remain in place over the region through

the near term period. With light winds, haze/smoke aloft will 

continue to linger through tonight, before gradually dispersing 

on Friday.

 

Isolated slow moving showers and thunderstorms have popped up 

once again on the backside of a departing upper level low. These

are terrain driven this afternoon and early evening and over 

the Catskills, closer to the upper low. Once the daytime heating

and instability decreases and the cumulus clouds fade away, it

will be mainly clear tonight. This will allow for patchy valley

fog to form once again late tonight into early Friday morning;

mainly in the valleys of the Catskills & Poconos where humidity

levels are forecast to be higher. Overnight lows dip down

between 55 to 65 areawide.



Friday is another quiet day, with some light haze or smoke aloft

still possible. Latest HRRR Smoke model guidance shows

concentrations of the vertically integrated smoke decreasing

over the area (much lower than today). Therefore, decided to

leave the mention of haze/smoke out of the official forecast at

this time...with the reasoning being that it will hardly be

noticeable by Friday afternoon. There could still be a stray,

isolated shower or t'storm over the Mohawk Valley region in the

afternoon...otherwise it will be sunny and warm with highs in

the upper 70s to mid-80s and low humidity.



Quiet, seasonably cool with valley fog expected Friday night as

high pressure remains in control. Lows will be in the 50s to low

60s areawide.



&&



.SHORT TERM /SATURDAY THROUGH SUNDAY NIGHT/...

120 PM Update...



High pressure continues through the short term period. High 

temperatures will trend slightly higher each day given the model

guidance warming of the boundary layer. Some locations may get 

close to 90 on Saturday, with temperatures increasing again for

Sunday, with quite a few places with potential for getting at or

into the 90s. Overnight lows increase slightly as well with 

some moisture being advected in as well. Will also be monitoring

the potential for morning valley fog. Recent dry conditions may

limit the possibility of fog. Given the recent dry pattern, a 

wide range in temperatures each day looks likely.



&&



.LONG TERM /MONDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...

120 PM Update...



Modeled boundary layer temperatures indicate most locations 

should get to 90 or in the low 90's early next week. The good 

news is that humidity does not look to get out of hand which 

means heat index values look only slightly higher and topping 

out just short of 95 in CNY. Given the recent dry pattern, a 

wide range in temperatures each day looks likely.



A weak cold front still looks probable for early next week, but

confidence is generally moderate for timing. For now, we went

with the NBM solution with slight chances for rain showers and

storms Tuesday afternoon



&&



.AVIATION /00Z FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...



Smoke and haze will help lead to some reduced visibilities at 

all of our NY terminals through tonight and early Friday

morning. Visibility will fluctuate between MVFR to VFR at times

through the overnight.



Light patches of fog are possible ELM late tonight with decent 

radiational cooling expected. Overnight lows will get close to

the crossover temperature, likely within a degree or two.



By mid-morning on Friday, the smoke and haze will be

dissipating...with widespread VFR conditions then expected

through the end of the taf period.



Outlook...



Friday afternoon through Tuesday...Mainly VFR except some early

morning valley fog possible at KELM.



&&



.BGM WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

PA...None.

NY...None.



&&



$$



SYNOPSIS...MJM/MWG

NEAR TERM...MJM

SHORT TERM...KL/MWG

LONG TERM...KL/MWG

AVIATION...ES/MJM



