[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.8950","issue":"2025-08-07T23:34:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS63","cccc":"KDMX","awipsid":"AFDDMX"}]
=================================================



681

FXUS63 KDMX 072334

AFDDMX



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Des Moines IA

634 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



 ...Updated for the 00z Aviation Discussion...



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Hot and humid on Friday with heat index values reaching 100

  degrees or higher.



- Repeated rounds of storms possible by late Saturday through

  early next week. Periods with severe storms and heavy rainfall

  may occur.



&&



.DISCUSSION...

Issued at 256 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



The morning thunderstorms ended by early this afternoon over 

southern Iowa as warmer air aloft has resulted in developing 

elevated mixed layer (EML). That EML will deter convection for much 

of Iowa over the next 24-36 hrs. That stated, numerous waves of 

theta-e advection will be lifting across Iowa during this time and 

ACCAS development above the EML is occurring over eastern 

Nebraska. So while expecting a dry forecast into Friday, a 

localized shower or two could occur if the ACCAS become more 

agitated.



A heat advisory has been issued for Friday afternoon for a large 

portion of Iowa. High temperatures in the lower 90s and dew points 

in the low to mid 70s will push heat index values into the 100s. 



An active forecast then returns for this weekend and into next week. 

A closed low will be moving from southern Alberta to southern 

Manitoba through early Saturday. That system will suppress the upper 

level high to the south and bring modified southwest flow into Iowa 

as a surface boundary settles into the state. That southwest flow 

will cause the boundary to stall of the state for much of the 

weekend and potentially as long as early next week with repeated 

rounds of storms occurring over Iowa. One immediate concern will be 

heavy rain potential with storm motions nearly parallel to the 

stalled boundary leading to training of storms and plentiful 

moisture available with pwats reaching 2" at times leading to 

efficient rain producing storms. Severe/organized storms will also 

be possible with good deep layer shear of 30+ kts. Will further 

analyze this potential with the upcoming forecasts. As noted, this 

pattern may persist into late Tue/early Wed until the primary short 

wave finally lifts through the state. The potential for significant 

rainfall is there for some areas from Saturday night through Tuesday 

night. Stay tuned to potential river and stream impacts along with 

Flash Flooding potential.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z SATURDAY/...

Issued at 633 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Areas of mid level clouds, with some low level clouds mixed in

further west will continue to remain over parts of the state tonight

into Friday morning, with a low potential for MVFR conditions 

over northern Iowa at times during this period. However, VFR 

conditions likely will remain across the sites through the 

period. Winds out of the SE will gradually turn more southerly 

through Friday, with breezy conditions by late morning as gusts 

between 20-25 knots are expected.



&&



.DMX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

Heat Advisory from 1 PM to 7 PM CDT Friday for IAZ023-033>035-

044>050-057>062-070>075-081>086-092>097.



&&



$$



DISCUSSION...Donavon

AVIATION...Bury



