[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.8968","issue":"2025-08-07T23:35:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS65","cccc":"KPSR","awipsid":"AFDPSR"}]
=================================================



699

FXUS65 KPSR 072335

AFDPSR



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Phoenix AZ

435 PM MST Thu Aug 7 2025



.UPDATE...Updated 00Z Aviation Discussion.



&&





.KEY MESSAGES...



- Extreme heat with readings around 10 degrees above normal and 

challenging record levels will gradually relax through the weekend.



- Extreme Heat Warnings remain in effect through Friday with 

widespread major to extreme HeatRisk.



- Chances for showers and thunderstorms will gradually increase 

across eastern Arizona high terrain with the best chances arriving 

over the weekend.



&&



.SHORT TERM /Today through Saturday/...

Objective analysis and 12Z raob data indicates midlevel subtropical 

ridging has changed little in the past 24 hours with an anomalous 

600dm H5 anti-cyclone centered over western New Mexico. Lower level 

thermal profiles have warmed under this high pressure system with 

analysis suggesting a pool of +31C H8 temperatures readily available 

for mechanical mixing resulting in record level readings today and 

widespread major to extreme HeatRisk. However, notable changes in 

the synoptic pattern and thermodynamic profiles will occur over the 

next 24-48 hours. First, deepening progressive troughing over the 

northern Rockies/Great Basin will help erode SW Conus subtropical H5 

heights closer to 594dm while also initiating a retrogressive phase. 

Concurrently, tropospheric thermal profiles will cool a few degrees 

while higher moisture levels limit the depth of the mixed boundary 

layer significantly reducing the expanse of major HeatRisk into the 

weekend. 



Secondly, overwhelming model evidence suggests the synoptic pattern 

and thermal gradient inducing a robust Gulf surge tonight such that 

surface dewpoints in the 60s and 70s will overspread much of the CWA 

lower elevations. Simultaneously, residual midlevel moisture should 

rotate around the larger anti-cyclone again tonight resulting in 

another round of morning ACCAS/virga/light showers. The enhanced low 

level moisture with mixing ratios around 10 g/kg will only extend 

through the sfc-H8 layer and partially scour with diurnal mixing 

resulting in another afternoon of large CinH across lower elevation 

communities. Confidence is good that thunderstorms will remain 

relegated to mountainous areas, though better organized and longer 

lived outflows may survive into lower deserts. Otherwise, will 

maintain the Extreme Heat Warning through Friday evening despite a 

large reduction in major HeatRisk given somewhat higher humidity 

levels counteracting the cooler temperatures during part of the day.



Forecast sounding data indicates another bump in moisture levels 

Saturday with slightly better than 10 g/kg mixing ratios and total 

column PWATs around 1.50" entering the conversation. However, given 

the thermal profiles aloft, this still presents an environment of 

substantial CinH limiting the ability for storms to survive off 

higher terrain. More likely, enhanced outflow boundaries will surge 

into lower elevations with only a very slight (~10% chance) of 

renewed development. Based on forecast sounding data, it would take 

multiple, deep outflows to mix then dislodge boundary layer moisture 

into saturation at steeper lapse rates aloft. Otherwise, high 

temperatures should cool another degree or two with HeatRisk mostly 

falling into a moderate category. Some localized major HeatRisk is 

still present in automated NBM forecasts though larger forecast 

uncertainty exists with respect to the influence of outflows and 

moisture surges on mixing efficiency. At this time, the areal 

coverage of major HeatRisk still looks too limited to necessitate 

further warnings, however numerical guidance does give equal weight 

towards temperatures being a couple degrees warmer than the mandated 

NBM output, so trends in model forecasts will need to be followed 

closely. 



&&



.LONG TERM /Sunday through Thursday/... 

By Sunday, a trough is forecast to track southeastward across the 

Northern Rockies and the Great Basin. This trough will further 

suppress the ridge over our region, while also eventually bringing 

dry northerly flow by Sunday afternoon. This drying on Sunday will 

hurt the convective potential across the lower deserts, but the NBM 

still shows 20-30% chances remaining across the eastern Arizona high 

terrain. As the drying continues into Monday, low level mixing 

ratios are likely to drop back to 7-8 g/kg lowering rain chances 

even further. Temperatures are expected to remain fairly stable 

through early next week with highs mostly between 107-113 degrees, 

just on the verge of reaching major HeatRisk across the lower 

deserts.



The latter half of next week may allow for a more favorable south-

southeasterly moist flow out of Mexico as guidance mostly shows the 

subtropical ridge center reforming to our northeast. It is still too 

early to know how much moisture may advect back into our region 

later next week, but there are at least some model members 

indicating decent monsoon moisture spreading into southern and 

central Arizona by around next Friday. 



&&



.AVIATION...Updated at 2335Z.



South Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:

No major aviation weather concerns under FEW to SCT passing mid to 

high level cloud decks can be expected throughout the TAF period. 

The overall wind pattern will continue to exhibit the typical 

diurnal tendencies with occasional afternoon/evening gusts near 20 

kts.  



Southeast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:

No major aviation weather concerns under a FEW passing mid to high

level cloud decks through the TAF period. At KIPL, winds will

shift out of the west this evening, shifting back to the southeast

early tomorrow morning. At KBLH, winds will fluctuate between the

west to southwest becoming southerly early tomorrow morning. Wind

speeds will remain under 10 kts at KIPL with some minor early

evening gusts at both TAF sites. Wind speeds will pick up to

around 15 kts with gusts up to 22kts by late tomorrow morning at 

KBLH.





&&



.FIRE WEATHER...

Very hot conditions with near record high temperatures will be 

common again today with modest cooling, albeit still with above 

normal temperatures arriving later in the week. A gradual moisture 

increase may allow some isolated, weak afternoon storms to initially 

form over the high terrain of far eastern districts yielding slight 

chances (~10%) for dry lightning and new wildfire starts the next 

couple days. Winds will follow typical diurnal patterns with 

afternoon upslope gusts to 15-25 mph. Afternoon minRHs between 8-12% 

should increase closer to a 15-20% range later in the week with poor 

overnight recovery improving to fair to good thresholds. As even 

better moisture moves into the area over the weekend, thunderstorm 

coverage should increase with a few storms and gusty outflow 

boundaries potentially reaching into the south-central Arizona lower 

deserts. 



&&



.CLIMATE...



Record high temperatures this week:



Date        Phoenix         Yuma         El Centro

----        -------         ----         ---------

Aug 7     112 in 2012    114 in 1962    117 in 1970

Aug 8     116 in 2012    115 in 1940    115 in 2012

 

&&



.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

AZ...Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM MST Friday for AZZ530>563.



CA...Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM PDT Friday for CAZ560>570.



&&



$$



SHORT TERM...18

LONG TERM...Kuhlman

AVIATION...Lojero/95

FIRE WEATHER...Kuhlman/18

CLIMATE...18



