715

FXUS65 KGJT 072336

AFDGJT



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Grand Junction CO

536 PM MDT Thu Aug 7 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Widespread critical fire weather conditions will continue

  through Saturday with Red Flag Warnings in place.



- High temperatures will remain above normal with afternoon wind

  gusts of 30 to 40 mph Friday. 



- Winds are expected to diminish some Saturday, but critical

  fire weather conditions continue.



- A slight cooling trend begins Saturday, with critical fire

  weather conditions receding Sunday.



&&



.SHORT TERM /THROUGH FRIDAY NIGHT/...

Issued at 258 PM MDT Thu Aug 7 2025



With a low pressure tracking over the Northern Rockies and high 

pressure centered over New Mexico our sits under the tight gradient 

aloft. These winds will mix down to the surface due to a warm and 

dry environment in place. There is enough midlevel mositure for a few 

showers in the southern mountains this afternoon, but chances for 

measurable rain are pretty low. Passing high clouds are also 

expected for the next few days. Wind gusts of 25-40 mph will be 

common through early evening. Winds decrease somewhat overnight, but 

higher elevations could continue to see gusty winds with that jet 

overhead. Tomorrow as the low pressure continues eastward a cold 

front approaches from the north. Wind gusts of 25-40 mph will be 

common once again, and with minimal moisture advection critical fire 

weather will be widespread. The front moves into the northern 

portions of the area tomorrow afternoon and evening. Winds behind 

the front will shift to the northwest and north. Models are hinting 

at a slight chance for showers along the front, but not much rain is 

expected if any at all. The showers could actually cause the winds 

to increase locally.



&&



.LONG TERM /SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...

Issued at 258 PM MDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Low pressure working across the northern CONUS early Saturday will 

continue to knock down the ridge of high pressure here in the 

Southwest. This will have little impact to the prevailing westerly 

flow aloft as we remain in broad cyclonic flow at that point. High 

temperatures will see a slight dip thanks to a cooler air mass that 

will help us remain closer to seasonal normal Saturday afternoon. 

Orographics on the edge of the Continental Divide could spark a few 

showers  on the terrain against the eastern edge of our CWA. Surface 

winds remain elevated enough Saturday to necessitate another Red 

Flag Warning thanks to the persistent low relative humidities. By 

Sunday, northwesterly flow on the backside of the trough is expected 

to relax upper level winds enough to keep us out of widespread 

critical fire weather conditions. This will also reinforce the 

cooling trend for a couple more days. More good news for suppressing 

critical fire weather. Models are hinting at a weak push of moisture 

into the southern CWA, mostly on the San Juans. This could produce 

some showers on Sunday afternoon. Wetting rains could be hard to 

come by yet with ensemble PWAT's in the 0.50 inch range across 

southwest Colorado. Unfortunately, this is about as good as it gets 

around the CWA this coming week. Shower chances remain confined to 

the San Juans and up the Divide into the Central Mountains, but 

moisture never does appreciably increase. By Tuesday night, we 

return to more subsident southwesterly flow and temperatures surge 

back into the above normal range to close out next week. 

Fortunately, it looks like surface winds remain below critical fire 

weather thresholds. However, continued low relative humidity thanks 

to an absence of monsoon moisture will make the threat of dry 

lightning something to watch for in the coming forecast periods.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z SATURDAY/...

Issued at 532 PM MDT Thu Aug 7 2025



VFR conditions are forecast through the TAF period. Gusty winds

will taper off after sunset before building back in Friday 

afternoon. Peak gusts of 25-35 knots are likely at most 

terminals. Smoke and haze may be visible at some terminals 

throughout the day, but we do not anticipate visibilities 

dropping below VFR thresholds.





&&



.FIRE WEATHER...

Issued at 258 PM MDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Overall the winds decrease tonight, but higher elevations could 

remain gusty due to the jet overhead. Wind gusts of 25-40 mph will 

be common tomorrow afternoon with low humidity once again. A cold 

front will enter the northern CWA in the afternoon and evening. A 

few showers are possible along the front, but increased wind is more 

likely than rainfall. Winds look to shift to the northwest and north 

behind the front. In the post frontal regime on Saturday winds will 

be more west to northwest. Gusts will not be as high as tomorrow, 

but near critical for many locations. Therefore Red Flag Warnings 

have been issued for several fire zones.



&&



.GJT WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...



CO...Red Flag Warning until 8 PM MDT this evening for COZ200>203-

     205-207-290-292>294.

     Red Flag Warning from 11 AM to 8 PM MDT Friday for COZ200>203-

     205-207-290-292>294.

     Red Flag Warning from 11 AM to 8 PM MDT Saturday for 

     COZ201>203-205-207-290-292>294.

UT...Red Flag Warning until 8 PM MDT this evening for UTZ485>487-

     490-491.

     Red Flag Warning from 11 AM to 8 PM MDT Friday for UTZ485>487-

     490-491.

     Red Flag Warning from 11 AM to 8 PM MDT Saturday for UTZ487-

     490-491.



&&



