[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.8987","issue":"2025-08-07T23:37:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS63","cccc":"KGRB","awipsid":"AFDGRB"}]
=================================================



719

FXUS63 KGRB 072337

AFDGRB



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Green Bay WI

637 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Updated aviation portion for 00Z TAF issuance



.KEY MESSAGES...



- There is a chance of thunderstorms for the rest of this

  afternoon through tonight, especially over central and east

  central Wisconsin. The strongest storms will be capable of

  producing gusty winds, torrential downpours, and small hail.



- Areas of fog, locally dense, may impact travel conditions over 

  northern Wisconsin late tonight.



- Heat and humidity will build into the region on Friday and

  Saturday, with heat indices expected to reach 95 to 100 in parts

  of central, east-central, and northeast Wisconsin.



- There will be a Slight Risk of severe thunderstorms Saturday

  afternoon and evening. Locally heavy rainfall will also

  possible. Additional thunderstorm chances will continue at 

  times through the rest of the weekend and the early to mid part 

  of the next work week.



- Increasing southerly winds and building waves may result in

  hazardous boating and swimming conditions on Lake Michigan from

  late Friday night through late Saturday night.



&&



.DISCUSSION...

Issued at 224 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Short Term...Tonight and Friday



The  latest RAP analysis and satellite/radar imagery show a warm 

front extending from central North Dakota, across southern 

Minnesota, to eastern Iowa early this afternoon. Weak shortwave 

energy or embedded MCVs are traversing the western Great Lakes; 

the most pronounced shortwave is located over Lake Superior. The 

airmass is very unstable north of the warm front as temperatures 

reach their convective temperatures, with most unstable capes of 

2000-3000 j/kg. Cumulus cloud coverage is robust across northeast 

Wisconsin this afternoon. A few showers developed by midday over 

central Wisconsin but are not showing signs of becoming more

widespread this afternoon despite further destabilization. 

Forcing is relatively weak aloft and along the warm front, so 

confidence is low that strong to severe storms will develop. But 

given the robust instability, a rogue strong storm cannot be 

ruled out later this afternoon through early evening. As the warm 

front lifts northeast across Wisconsin, forecast concerns revolve 

around thunderstorm potential and excessive heat.



Thunderstorms



Shortwave ridging will be building into Wisconsin over the next 

24 hours. The unstable airmass will continue to be present tonight

as a warm front approaches from the southwest. The chances of 

thunderstorms will remain low (below 40%). Most guidance places 

the highest chances over eastern Wisconsin where weak shortwave 

energy will be present. With mu capes of 1000-2000 j/kg, it is 

possible that a rogue strong storm could develop prior to the mid-

level cap building into the region late tonight. Kept a small 

chance of showers and storms over eastern Wisconsin on Friday 

morning (less than 20%). The rest of the day should be capped as 

700 mb temperatures warm to 12-13 degrees Celsius.



Heat



A very warm and muggy airmass will move into the region for 

Friday. High temperatures in the upper 80s to lower 90s can be 

expected over most of the area. Combined with the humidity, heat 

indices will be in the middle to upper 90s for much of central and

northeast Wisconsin away from the Bay and Lake. After 

coordinating with surrounding offices, a decision was made to hold

off on issuing a Heat Advisory due to concerns about how long 

overnight storms could possibly linger into Friday.



Fog



Guidance is picking up on the potential for dense fog over far

northern WI late tonight. Increasing moisture along a warm front

will help fog formation. Locally dense fog is possible so will

continue to advertise in the HWO.



Long Term...Friday Night Through Thursday



Thunderstorms



The weather pattern over North America will consist of an upper 

trough from central Canada to the southern Plains, while 

subtropical ridging will extend from the Gulf Coast to eastern 

Canada. This pattern will place the western Great Lakes within a 

southwest flow aloft and the corridor of above-normal temperatures

at 850 mb for much of the next week. The focus in this part of 

the forecast remains on excessive heat along with the potential 

for severe thunderstorms through the weekend. 



The atmosphere will be capped on Friday night thanks to 700 mb 

temperatures in the 12-14 degrees Celsius range. The cap erodes on

Saturday as a cold front approaches from the northwest. An 

instability axis of 2000-3000 j/kg will arrive ahead of the slow-

moving boundary along with precipitable water values around the 

97.5 to 99 percentile (pwats around 2 inches) on Saturday and 

Sunday during peak heating. Effective shear has seen an uptick in 

this forecast cycle (20-30 kts), which increases the risk of 

organized severe weather. High instability combined with ample 

moisture should lead to a wind and flash flooding threat from 

training, slow-moving storms. Repeated rounds of storms could also

pose a flooding risk given the juicy environment on Sunday. This 

should be more of an issue over eastern Wisconsin where storms 

are expected to be more concentrated. 



This cold front will shift east of the area on Monday, which 

could offer a modest reprieve from the heat, humidity, and 

thunderstorm potential. But then it will return north by Tuesday 

or Wednesday, leading to renewed chances of thunderstorms.



Heat



The heat and humidity will continue on Friday night and Saturday.

Lows on Friday night will likely remain in the 70s and offer 

little in the way of relief. Highs in the middle 80s to low 90s 

will bring heat indices into the middle to upper 90s on Saturday 

over northeast Wisconsin. A heat advisory may be needed, with heat

indices approaching advisory criteria on Saturday afternoon. 

Temperatures look to remain above normal for much of the next 

week.



Marine



A period of conditions hazardous to small craft will be possible 

on Lake Michigan from late Friday night through late Saturday

night. Conditions will also be favorable for rip currents, and a 

Beach Hazard Statement may be needed.



&&



.AVIATION...for 00Z TAF Issuance

Issued at 637 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



FEW-SCT afternoon cumulus clouds were noted with some high level

clouds streaming in at times. Despite an unstable atmosphere in

place, showers and thunderstorms are not expected as CAMs are

mostly dry. Therefore, will maintain the dry forecast tonight. 



Attention turns to possible significant fog development over

mainly northern WI late tonight. Could see widespread IFR/LIFR

conditions over the Northwoods, including the RHI TAF site,

between 07z-12z/Fri. The low ceilings will likely be slow to lift

due to a strong inversion on Friday morning.



A mainly dry forecast is again expected on Friday as CAMs models

are once again mostly devoid of showers and thunderstorms across

northern Wisconsin. Some mid and high clouds will again be

possible, especially during Friday afternoon.



&&



.GRB WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

None.



&&



$$



DISCUSSION.....MPC

AVIATION.......Kurimski



