2025-08-07T23-37-27-565Z

[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.8988","issue":"2025-08-07T23:37:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS63","cccc":"KILX","awipsid":"AFDILX"}]
=================================================



720

FXUS63 KILX 072337

AFDILX



Area Forecast Discussion...Updated Aviation

National Weather Service Lincoln IL

637 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- While scattered thunderstorms are expected late afternoon into

  evening (mainly east of I-55), the severe weather threat is

  lowering. 



- Seasonably hot/humid conditions are expected through the

  weekend, but increased cloud cover and showers/storms early next

  week will help suppress high temperatures Monday through

  Thursday. 



&&



.SHORT TERM...  (Tonight and Friday)

Issued at 203 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Still seeing showers and a few storms across west central Illinois

early this afternoon, with a remnant MCV from the earlier

convective cluster west of Quincy. The associated cloud cover has

put a dent in temperatures west of the Illinois River so far, 

struggling to reach 80, while much of the remainder of the CWA is 

seeing seasonably warm temps in the mid to upper 80s. 



Main focus for convection this afternoon and evening appears to be

in areas near and east of I-55. Being ahead of the cirrus shield,

surface CAPE's have reached over 2000 J/kg per latest SPC

mesoanalysis. However, shear is generally less than 20 knots. Most

recent SPC Day1 outlook removed the level 1 severe risk, but a few

of the storms may still briefly get strong. Some disagreement

noted amongst the high-res models beyond this evening, with a few

lingering showers past midnight.



Any residual cloud cover should thin out Friday morning, allowing

for a seasonably hot day with highs mainly in the lower 90s. Some

concern for potential for approaching heat advisory criteria 

across the Illinois River valley. However, HREF probabilities of 

heat index over 105 are quite low, so will hold off on an advisory

at this point. 



.LONG TERM...  (Saturday through Next Thursday)

Issued at 203 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Global models are in good agreement that an upper low, currently 

across Washington and British Columbia, will make it about as far

as Lake Winnipeg early this weekend. However, any further progress

will be stifled as the upper ridge shifts into the Midwest and

eastern Great Lakes region. This ridge sticks around for a few

days as low pressure forms east of New England. Initially, chances

for showers and storms will be more favored near and northwest of

I-55 this weekend, closer to the surface boundary. However, as we

get into early next week, pop-up storms become more of an issue

further southeast. 



Temperatures start off seasonably hot for the weekend, with highs

in the lower 90s and heat index potentially near advisory level

across the Illinois River valley. However, highs gradually ease

back early next week as cloud cover begins to increase.



Geelhart



&&



.AVIATION...  (For the 00z TAFs through 00z Friday Evening)

Issued at 636 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Convection has developed near DEC along a boundary that is slowly

moving east this evening. This boundary appears to be east of PIA,

BMI, and SPI with lighter winds at these locations and only

cirrus. Storms are between 5-10 nm of DEC so have VCTS and a short

1hr tempo group to cover. Since boundary is west of CMI, have kept

a PROB30 group for a few hours encase something pops up and moves

toward the site. All sites will have broken clouds remainder of

the evening and overnight with AC at DEC and CMI...with cirrus at

PIA, BMI, and SPI. Clouds will become scattered to few during the

morning and then some a little bit of cu possible tomorrow

afternoon. Light winds at all sites overnight with winds becoming

southerly tomorrow and some afternoon gusts around 20kts. 



Auten



&&



.ILX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

None.

&&



$$



