726

FXUS63 KMQT 072338

AFDMQT



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Marquette MI

738 PM EDT Thu Aug 7 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Isolated and scattered thunderstorms are possible central and

  east this afternoon through tonight.



- Heat and humidity increase into the weekend. The chance of

  heat indices exceeding 90 degrees is greater than 50% Friday

  and Saturday for most areas, with parts of the central and 

  western UP approaching 100 degree heat indices Saturday.



- Episodic showers and thunderstorms will be possible again

  Friday through the weekend (20% chance Friday, mainly in the

  afternoon, and 30-56% chance on the weekend).



&&



.SHORT TERM /THROUGH FRIDAY/...

Issued at 217 PM EDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Shortwave pushed east this morning across Lake Superior, supporting 

showers and thunderstorms that gradually coalesced into a line as 

they moved through. This line continues to focus shower and 

thunderstorm activity near Lake Superior in Alger and Luce counties. 

Some haze has been reported by surface observations here and there; 

overall though, its been a quiet day so far with temperatures 

climbing into the upper 70s and low 80s, dewpoints in the mid to 

upper 60s, and increasing cloud cover thanks to day time heating and 

the passing wave. 



Given the warm and humid conditions over the area, instability will 

continue to increase to near 2k j/kg this afternoon. With the 

afternoon lake breeze assisting development alongside deep layer 

shear near 25-30kts, showers and thunderstorms may develop along the 

boundary. Severe weather isn't expected, but heavy downpours and 

some hail will be possible should something organize well enough. 

Outside of this, the ongoing showers and thunderstorms in the east 

may continue for the next few hours, but should progress eastward 

later this afternoon. Otherwise, southerly flow will continue to 

advect in a warm and moist airmass into the area, which should push 

most of the region into the low to mid 80s. 



Tonight and Friday, continued warm air and moisture advection will 

only allow temperatures to cool into the mid 60s interior and upper 

60s lakeside. 850mb temperatures climb to near 20c late tonight, 

which with daytime heating Friday, widespread mid to upper 80 degree 

temperatures are expected. Low 90s can't be ruled out in the 

downslope prone locations of the interior west. This, alongside 

dewpoints climbing into the 70s will make it uncomfortable outside 

and pose a moderate to major heat risk, per NWS HeatRisk, for those 

without sufficient cooling or hydration. Anyone with outdoor plans 

Friday should ensure they take frequent breaks in the shade or 

inside and drink plenty of water. 



A weak impulse dropping southeast through the Upper Great Lakes may 

enable additional shower and storm development from near Menominee 

northeastward tonight, but I've not much confidence in this 

occuring. Another wave will press west to east through the region 

Friday. As is typical for these setups, the various deterministic 

outputs have a poor handle on timing as this feature transits the 

area. Given this, confidence is low that showers or storms will 

press through the region through the day. Should these occur though 

(25% or less), they'd be most likely across the west half of Upper 

Michigan in the afternoon



&&



.LONG TERM /FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...

Issued at 217 PM EDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Friday night, Upper Michigan will find itself under the ridge axis 

with an upper level low moving into southern Saskatchewan. To the 

south and southeast of this feature, multiple shortwaves are present 

in deterministic guidance rotating through the base toward the 

northeast while at the surface, a cold front stretches south through 

the eastern Dakotas/Minnesota. Ahead of this feature, southerly flow 

will continue to stretch across our forecast area, sustaining the 

very warm and humid airmass overhead. The result will be a strongly 

unstable atmosphere, with MUCAPE values anticipated between 2000 and 

3000 j/kg per rough deterministic consensus. Upstream MCS/QLCS 

development is expected earlier in the day, which will then 

propagate downstream into the Upper Great Lakes. Still high 

uncertainty about where said activity will end up traveling across, 

but latest CAMS suggest activity may be largely confined to Lake 

Superior and Wisconsin, save for isolated or scattered convection 

moving west to east through Upper Michigan. Deterministic suite 

suggests the environment will include a nocturnal 925-850mb 25-35kt 

LLJ which may provide enough low-mid level shear to sustain elevated 

shower and thunderstorm activity as it moves into our region 

overnight. Despite the level of instability, strong to severe 

thunderstorms aren't expected outside of areas close to Minnesota 

given the dearth of deep layer shear over Upper Michigan. However, 

hail can't be ruled out should an organized storm sustain itself 

well enough. Additionally, PWATS near 1.75 inches, corfidi vectors 

generally 10-20kts, and a deep warm cloud layer above 1000k feet 

suggest heavy downpours will be possible. Expecting a mild night 

otherwise with many areas in the west and near the lakeshores 

potentially only cooling into the low 70s while the east dips into 

the mid to upper 60s. 



The cold front is expected to press into the forecast area late 

Saturday. Morning thunderstorm chances will be dependent on the 

timing and location of activity overnight Friday. Regardless, of 

this though, afternoon temperatures are still anticipated to surge 

into the upper 80s to low 90s while dewpoints hover in the low 70s, 

resulting in heat indices climbing into the 90s for most, if not all 

locations of Upper Michigan. Still some questions as to how high 

indices will climb, but there has been a multi-day consistent signal 

for the potential of moderate to major heat risk levels for the 

region, per NWS HeatRisk. This is especially relevant for anyone 

with outdoor plans or those without adequate cooling/hydration. 

Gusty southerly winds are also expected across the region, but 

potentially near 30kts across eastern Upper Michigan. This is likely 

to create dangerous swimming conditions for Lake Michigan beaches. 

By afternoon, showers and thunderstorms tied to the advancing cold 

front will move into the west. Timing favors late afternoon or 

evening for initial precip, but it should be noted this varies with 

solution. Thunderstorm activity will progress eastward ahead of the 

boundary into the evening hours. Similarly to Friday night, the 

environment will support heavy rain potential with these 

thunderstorms and there is some risk (Marginal or 1 of 5, per SPC) 

for strong to severe thunderstorms given the steep mid-level lapse 

rates and increasing deep layer shear. Should this materialize, the 

severe weather threats will be strong to damaging winds and large 

hail.



The cold front will slowly press west to east through the region 

Sunday into early Monday as the upper level low lifts northeast into 

Ontario and Hudson Bay. Similarly to the previous waves, timing is 

uncertain and could be the focus for additional shower and 

thunderstorm activity, mainly for central and eastern Upper 

Michigan. The pattern gradually takes on a more zonal look Monday 

into Tuesday and then weak mid-level ridging for the rest of the 

week. There continues to be near daily chances for rain and 

thunderstorms given the potential for impulses moving through the 

flow. Cooler air will eventually press into the region behind the 

front, but still expect widespread mid to upper 80s on Sunday with 

near 80F to the mid 80s thereafter next week.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z SATURDAY/...

Issued at 737 PM EDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Predominantly VFR conditions are expected through the duration of 

the 0Z TAF period. The exception to this will be some BR 

development, mainly at SAW, lowering vis down to MVFR late tonight 

with periods of IFR vis not out of the question (~25% chance). This 

would likely be accompanied by FEW/SCT lower cigs. Otherwise TSRA 

was removed at SAW as convective development along the outflow 

boundary has been struggling this afternoon and the cu field is 

weakening. Winds remain light, mainly 10 kts or less.



&&



.MARINE...

Issued at 217 PM EDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Light winds of 20 knots or less hold across Lake Superior the rest 

of the work-week as remnant high pressure ridging retreats and gives 

way to a more zonal pattern aloft. As a shortwave low moves through 

the Upper Great Lakes on Saturday, the winds could potentially 

increase from the south up to 25 knots, continuing Sunday between the 

Keweenaw and Thunder Bay before dropping back down to 20 knots or 

less again early next week. 



As for thunder chances, some showers and storms linger across the 

far eastern portions of the lake this afternoon. A brief reprieve in 

convection is expected into early Friday. Then, the shortwave 

lifting from the Plains moves just west of the region on Saturday; 

this will bring chances for showers and storms late Friday night 

through Saturday, with some of the storms potentially being strong 

over the western lake. Thunder chances return over the eastern lake 

Sunday, with low (~20%) storm chances continuing across the lake 

through early next week.



&&



.MQT WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

Upper Michigan...

None.

Lake Superior...

None.

Lake Michigan...

None.



&&



$$



SHORT TERM...JTP

LONG TERM...JTP

AVIATION...Jablonski

MARINE...LC



