2025-08-07T23-39-57-573Z

[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.9010","issue":"2025-08-07T23:39:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS62","cccc":"KJAX","awipsid":"AFDJAX"}]
=================================================



742

FXUS62 KJAX 072339

AFDJAX



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Jacksonville FL

739 PM EDT Thu Aug 7 2025



...New AVIATION...



.NEAR TERM...

Issued at 1249 PM EDT Thu Aug 7 2025



For the latest NE FL and SE GA Daily Key Messages please visit:

https://www.weather.gov/media/jax/briefings/nws-jax-briefing.pdf



Frontal zone will sink a little south into north central FL through

Tonight, as high pressure builds to the north. Showers and 

thunderstorms will continue through the afternoon, and diminish 

during the evening with loss of diurnal heating. With convergence

remaining with the boundary, shower chances will persist into the

overnight. Lows will range from the lower 70s inland to the mid 70s 

coast. 



&&



.SHORT TERM...

(Friday through Saturday night)

Issued at 1249 PM EDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Diurnal bouts of showers and storms are expected to form on Friday

and Saturday as the mid level trough ahead of the mostly

stationary frontal boundary over the region gradually drifts

northward by the end of the forecast period followed by a moist

southeasterly flow with PWAT values measuring upwards of 2.3

inches. Potential for numerous to widespread storms building

during this period with the primary drivers for developments

through the end of the week occurring along the sea breeze

boundaries and areas of convergence. High temperatures through the

end of the week and into the weekend will range between the upper

80s and lower 90s with warmer temperatures occurring to the south

of the I-10 corridor.



&&



.LONG TERM...

(Sunday through next Thursday)

Issued at 1249 PM EDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Moist onshore prevailing flow will be in place for Sunday and

Monday resulting in more widespread convection for the beginning

of next week with convection becoming more inhibited by midweek as

high pressure ridging extends in over the region from out of the

Atlantic. Temperatures will experience a warming trend starting

with the end of the weekend and continuing through into next week

with daily high temps expected to rise into the mid 90s by

midweek.



&&



.AVIATION...

(00Z TAFS)

Issued at 725 PM EDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Convection has avoided most airfields this afternoon and evening,

as only GNV has a small chance for impacts over the next few

hours, though not enough to include in the current forecast.

Convection inland will wane through about 02Z, with the focus then

shifting towards coastal sites tonight. MVFR cloud decks continue

to stream onshore, which have mostly stayed below BKN levels and

therefore predominantly VFR conditions. This will continue

overnight near the coast, with chances for SHRA also increasing

overnight with the stalled front and coastal trough. Brief periods

of MVFR are possible late this evening and overnight, though 

confidence not high enough to include any TEMPO groups in the 

forecast at this time. In addition to SHRA overnight, chances for 

TSRA also exist as shown by several hi-res guidance sources, 

mainly at the immediate coastline, i.e. at SGJ and SSI. 

Uncertainty is high with respect to these TSRA impacts however, 

and therefore have kept PROB30 groups at these same airfields 

accordingly in lieu of TEMPO. Stay tuned through this evening and 

overnight for forecast amendments. 



&&



.MARINE...

Issued at 1249 PM EDT Thu Aug 7 2025



A nearly stationary frontal boundary will be over the area through 

Friday. An area of low pressure may develop along this boundary off 

the southeastern US coast. This low would then move northeast of the

region over the weekend, as troughing extends southwest over area 

waters. High pressure will build to the northeast early next week. 



Rip Currents: Moderate through Friday



&&



.PRELIMINARY POINT TEMPS/POPS...

AMG  70  88  71  87 /  10  50  30  80 

SSI  76  86  77  87 /  30  70  70  90 

JAX  74  89  74  89 /  20  80  60  90 

SGJ  77  88  76  87 /  40  90  70  90 

GNV  73  89  74  90 /  30  90  40  90 

OCF  74  89  74  90 /  30  90  40 100 



&&



.JAX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

FL...None.

GA...None.

AM...None.

&&



