[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.9014","issue":"2025-08-07T23:39:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS61","cccc":"KALY","awipsid":"AFDALY"}]
=================================================



746

FXUS61 KALY 072339

AFDALY



AREA FORECAST DISCUSSION

National Weather Service Albany NY

739 PM EDT Thu Aug 7 2025



.SYNOPSIS...

Isolated to scattered afternoon showers and thunderstorms today

and tomorrow provide the only chances for precipitation over the

next several days as high pressure continues to dominate. The

work week and first half of the weekend will see the 

continuation of comfortable temperatures, but yet another warm 

up and increase in humidity begins Sunday through at least 

Wednesday.



&&



.DISCUSSION...

Key Messages: 



- Gradually increasing humidity accompanies warming temperatures

  Sunday through Wednesday, potentially warranting future Heat

  Advisories. 



The vast majority of the next several days will see a

continuance of the benign pattern we have found ourselves in

over the last several days. That said, some areas across the higher

terrain regions of the Southwest Adirondacks, Berkshires,

Southern Greens, and Eastern Catskills won't exactly escape the

end of the work week without any additional precipitation than

what accumulated yesterday. Modest instability and orographic

lift in these areas have worked in tandem to overcome subsidence

provided by the high such that pin-head showers and 

thunderstorms have been pulsing up and down throughout much of 

the morning. While no storms are anticipated to become severe

throughout the course of the afternoon, locally heavy downpours

have been noted through MRMS and Dual-Pol radar products so

localized ponding of water, especially in lower-lying and poor

drainage areas within smaller valleys embedded across the 

higher terrain cannot be ruled out. Tomorrow, too, will see 

isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms, primarily 

limited to areas north of Albany, especially the upslope regions

of the Southwest Adirondacks, as a northern- stream shortwave 

traverses southern Quebec and forces the pre- existing ridge to 

depart to our east. Surface high pressure will remain in place, 

however, and force all other areas to remain dry. 



Tomorrow night, as diurnal heating is lost and the shortwave

swiftly departs to the east, upper-level heights will rise once

again in the face of a ridge building eastward from the Midwest.

The amplifying ridge will continue to slide eastward through the

day Saturday, translating to a strengthening of the anticyclone

at the surface. Regionwide dry conditions will subsequently

remain in place through at least Tuesday afternoon as the ridge

and associated surface high remain locked in place by troughing

to the west and a coastal low pressure system stalling in the 

western Atlantic. The important thing to note throughout this 

time period is the increasing temperatures and humidity. 

Beginning Sunday, flow begins to prevail out of the southwest, 

advecting Gulf moisture into the region. With the surface high 

and ridge aloft retaining their strength, if not amplifying 

slightly, through Tuesday, temperatures will once again rise 

into the mid 80s to low 90s with dewpoints increasing to the mid

to upper 60s. Presently, Monday and Tuesday look to be the most

uncomfortable days with heat index values reaching the low to 

pockets of upper 90s, so Heat Advisories may be in the future of

some valley areas. 



The next chance for potentially widespread precipitation comes

Tuesday night into Wednesday as a cold front passes through the

region. However, the details surrounding this portion of the

forecast are still fairly uncertain given the extensive lead

time and model differences.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...

Flying conditions are currently VFR for all sites. There has

been some scattered convection around the region today, but this

activity is beginning to wane with the loss of daytime heating.

Based on radar imagery, will still mention a VCSH for KALB/KGFL

for the next two hours, but activity should be winding down and

impact on visibility is not anticipated at the TAF sites.  



Decreasing cloud cover is expected overnight with winds becoming

very light to calm. Some fog may develop at KGFL/KPSF towards

daybreak, which could allow for a brief period of MVFR/IFR

visibility at these sites. Otherwise, it will stay VFR for all

sites overnight. Any fog or mist will dissipate shortly after

daybreak.



Flying conditions should be VFR during the day on Friday with 

no precip expected. Just few-sct cumulus around 4-6 kft is 

expected with light southerly winds. 



Outlook...



Friday Night: No Operational Impact. NO SIG WX.

Saturday: No Operational Impact. NO SIG WX.

Saturday Night: No Operational Impact. NO SIG WX.

Sunday: No Operational Impact. NO SIG WX.

Sunday Night: No Operational Impact. NO SIG WX.

Monday: No Operational Impact. NO SIG WX.

Monday Night: No Operational Impact. NO SIG WX.

Tuesday: No Operational Impact. NO SIG WX.



&&



.ALY WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

CT...None.

NY...None.

MA...None.

VT...None.



&&



