761

FXUS61 KILN 072340

AFDILN



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Wilmington OH

740 PM EDT Thu Aug 7 2025



.SYNOPSIS...

The quiet pattern continues through the end of the work week and 

into the weekend, with isolated afternoon thunderstorms possible 

each day. Temperatures and moisture both increase over the next 

several days.



&&



.NEAR TERM /UNTIL 7 AM FRIDAY MORNING/...

Isolated to scattered showers and storms will continue into the

evening hours before dissipating overnight. There will be the

potential for some patchy fog primarily around the tri state region 

and river valley locations. Lows in the 60s are generally expected.



&&



.SHORT TERM /7 AM FRIDAY MORNING THROUGH FRIDAY NIGHT/...

The broad upper level ridges both to our west and to our northeast 

continue to dominate the pattern. The ILN CWA will be situated on 

the southwest side of the New England surface high, allowing for 

southerly return flow to drive into the region. Friday high 

temperatures are forecast to be nearly normal, if not slightly 

above, with temperatures reaching the upper 80s, possibly tickling 

90 for areas along the Ohio River. 



Once again, cannot rule out a stray summertime thunderstorm, 

however, forcing is nearly non-existant so not expecting anything 

other than some heavy rain and lightning with any pop ups. 



Overnight, we remain quiet. Low temperatures fall to the upper 60s.



&&



.LONG TERM /SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...

Through the weekend and into early next week, much of the region 

will remain placed in between two areas of above normal PWATs. With 

no major forcing mechanisms, persistent weather can be expected 

through this period. Highs are above normal, generally in the upper 

80s to lower 90s. Isolated showers and a few thunderstorms can't be 

ruled during the afternoon hours. 



A noticeable trend has taken shape over the last few model cycles 

regarding the trough that was expected to move through the Midwest 

and Great Lakes Tuesday and Wednesday. Instead of a sharp trough, 

much of the ensemble guidance has trended toward a more persistent 

ridge with a weak trough glancing through the region. This creates 

some uncertainty regarding the placement of the cold front that was 

originally supposed to move into the area on Tuesday. Even if the 

front is lacking, above normal PWATs will advect northeastward as 

deep southwesterly flow settles in over the Ohio Valley midweek. 

This would support a more active thunderstorm pattern with locally 

heavy rainfall threats returning for a few days into late next week.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...

Have VCSH at KLUK, KCMH, and KLCK at the very start of the TAF

period, and a TSRA at KCVG. Otherwise have dry conditions at the area

TAF sites. Expect VLIFR conditions to develop at KLUK with river

valley fog overnight. Just have tempo MVFR vsbys at KILN late in the

overnight hours. Additional cu are expected tomorrow. Winds will

generally be light and less than 10 knots except for the chance for

some thunderstorm outflow near the start of the TAF period around

KCVG and KLUK.



OUTLOOK...Thunderstorms are possible on Tuesday.



&&



.ILN WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

OH...None.

KY...None.

IN...None.



&&



$$



SYNOPSIS...CA

NEAR TERM...

SHORT TERM...CA

LONG TERM...McGinnis

AVIATION...



