953

FXUS63 KABR 072343

AFDABR



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Aberdeen SD

643 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- A Heat Advisory remains in effect for portions of central South

  Dakota through this evening as heat indices could reach as high

  as 104 degrees.



- An active pattern continues with showers and thunderstorms (30-

  45%) in the forecast through early Saturday morning, mainly for

  portions of north central through northeastern SD into west 

  central MN.



- There is an Enhanced Risk (level 3 out of 5) for severe storms

  north of a line from Eureka to Britton and a slight risk (level

  2 out of 5) for severe weather north of Highway 14 this evening

  through tonight. The main threat will be wind gusts of 60-70 

  mph and quarter sized hail. There is a 10% chance of wind gusts 

  exceeding 74 mph north of Highway 12. 



- There is a slight risk (level 2 out of 5) for severe weather

  over the James River Valley and eastward into west central MN 

  Friday afternoon through Friday night. All hazards are possible 

  including tornadoes.



&&



.DISCUSSION...

Issued at 303 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



As of 200pm, cumulus clouds cover most of the forecast area moving 

from the southeast to the northwest with a few high clouds pushing 

east over north central SD. RAP indicates the axis of the ridge is 

directly overhead and it feels like it as temps are ranging in the 

80s to around 90 and dewpoints in the lower to mid 70s (with help 

from the corn) so it is quite humid out there. A Heat Advisory is in 

effect for portions of central SD has heat indices could top out 

between 100-104 degrees.



The ridge will continue to push east as the mid level low will 

center itself over WA/ID border by 00Z with the axis of its trough 

extending southward. This will put the Northern Plains in southwest 

flow aloft on the PVA side of this trough. Within this flow, several 

embedded shortwaves/pulses will move in over the region this evening 

into tonight as this system will continue to shift eastward and 

closer to the region through the overnight. It does look like the 

better shortwave activity will mainly be in ND through the ND/SD 

border. At the surface, models are pretty consistent on the center 

of the lee low hovering over western SD along its southwest to 

northeast oriented boundary stretching through ND by 00Z. This low 

will then shift a bit eastward and over west central SD through the 

overnight and its trailing cold front to the southwest. Within the 

warm sector and southwest flow at the surface, dewpoints will 

continue in the lower to mid 70s. However, the CAP that has been in 

place today, due to the warm temps at 700, will start to erode 

around to little after sunset (north of highway 14) along with the 

low level jet increasing in strength out ahead of this low, allowing 

for convection. 



There are 2 different scenarios depending on what model you look at 

on what will occur convection wise. The first scenario is storms 

initiating over eastern MT to western ND this afternoon with the 

system quickly growing upscale into a line and eventually into a 

bowing line as it tracks east through ND and eventually into MN. 

Around 02Z, the southern edge of this line of storms looks to clip 

along the ND/SD border, as the line track east, with the possibility 

of cold pooling/outflow portion of the line taking more of a 

southeastern track into northern to northeastern SD and west central 

MN, as it will want to track into the better instability, with the 

possibility of additional convection behind this line. This is shown 

by HRW NSSl, HRW ARW, and NAM NEST. However, FV3 show less of a cold 

pooling and keeps everything well north. HRRR has the southern edge 

of this line clipping along the ND/SD border (along with more storms 

pushing eastward behind it out of MT and also could bow, making a 

double bow scenario over ND) with the outflow dominant part moving 

southeast but into far northeastern SD or western MN, but until 

early Friday morning along with additional spotty convection behind 

it. Corfidi vectors indicate northwest to southeast motion between 

00-03Z then north to south storm motion around 06Z. So in conclusion 

all CAMs agree on some sort of bowing linear line trucking eastward 

across ND but the outcomes are different on how far this line forms 

southward into our CWA and if we get enough cold pooling/outflow 

within this line for this portion to track southeastward into our 

northern and northeastern CWA. Whatever outcome, the atmosphere will 

be primed for any convection to become severe as MUCAPE will be on 

the order of 3000-4500 j/kg with max values potentially up into the 

5000j/kg range and decreasing through the overnight. We also have 

ample bulk shear of 30-40kts and lapse rates between 8-8.5C/km per 

HREF between 00-06Z Friday. If we get this outflow/cold pooling 

portion of the line, then damaging winds would be the main threat. 

Probability of severe wind is hatched north of highway 12 meaning a 

10% probability of wind gusts over 74 mph. If we get more broken 

lines/clusters ahead or behind the main line then large hail/winds 

is also possible. This storms within this MCS looks to exit the far 

eastern half of SD by Friday morning. There is an Enhanced Risk 

(level 3 out of 5) north of a line from Eureka to Britton (for

this prob of higher wind) and a slight risk (level 2 out of 5) 

north of Highway 14 this evening through tonight 



With these embedded shortwave pulses, rounds of weak showers and 

thunderstorms could continue over the northern portions of SD 

through the day. Our attention then turns to Friday afternoon into 

Friday night as the mid level low will hover over the MT/ND border 

into Canada with strong vorticity advection around the low and 

ongoing southwesterly flow on the PVA side of its trough over the 

Dakotas. Mid level winds increase to 50kts on the tail of the mid 

level jet over northeastern SD/western MN Friday night. The lee low 

will track east then northeast in ND through the day with the cold 

front sweeping through and positioned over the SD/MN border by 

Friday evening. Once again temps ahead of the cold front will range 

in the 80s to lower 90s and dewpoints in the upper 60s to the mid 

70s. With new runs of models, the front is forecasted to push 

eastward faster than previous forecast with this convection now 

mainly post frontal with storm mode with linear and clusters 

possible. We will lose that stronger CAPE, however, shear will still 

be strong between 40-60kts with straight hodographs given a more 

elevated hail/wind potential. SPC has downgraded the enhanced that 

was over far northeastern SD back down to a slight risk and covers 

James River Valley and eastward given this possible post frontal 

uncertainty. 



Temps will be cooler ranging in the upper 70s to the mid/upper 80s 

through Sunday within a troughing pattern over the area. By early 

next week warmer air filters in from the west/southwest and will 

help temps rise back up into the 80s/90s through the end of next 

week. Also, much drier conditons look to setup next week with 

overall dry conditions Saturday afternoon through the middle of next 

week.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z FRIDAY/...

Issued at 643 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Terminals KABR,KATY,KPIR,KMBG



VFR to MVFR conditions are expected. While there is still a chance

of storms late tonight into Friday morning, the potential is

lowering with time as thunderstorms upstream still need to develop

or near the area. MVFR ceilings are most likely at MBG mid to late

morning Friday. LLWS is expected at PIR with winds just above the

surface mainly out of the south around 35kts from 06-13Z Friday.



&&



.ABR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

SD...Heat Advisory until 8 PM CDT /7 PM MDT/ this evening for SDZ015-

     033>035-045-048-051.



MN...None.

&&



$$



DISCUSSION...MMM

AVIATION...06



