=================================================



040

FXUS63 KMPX 072345

AFDMPX



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Twin Cities/Chanhassen MN

645 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Potential for strong to severe thunderstorms capable of 

  damaging wind overnight across central & eastern Minnesota.



- Hot & humid Friday with heat indices potentially rising to 

  around 100 degrees.



- Another complex of severe thunderstorms is possible overnight

  Friday into saturday morning.



&&



.DISCUSSION...

Issued at 225 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Main forecast concern tonight regards the track & timing of a  

complex of thunderstorms expected to develop across North Dakota

this evening. These thunderstorms look to develop into a 

classic mesoscale convective system overnight capable of 

producing damaging wind gusts, with the question being where 

does the complex track as it moves eastwards through Minnesota. 

High-resolution models remain generally mixed on either tracking

the complex due east across northern Minnesota, potentially 

clipping our central Minnesota counties (Douglas-Kanabec), or 

diving the complex southeastwards along Interstate 94 & towards 

the Twin Cities metro after sunrise. A defined northwest to 

southeast instability gradient will be present late tonight 

across western & central Minnesota, along which these systems 

will generally propagate along, although this gradient becomes 

less-pronounced as the instability weakens through early 

tomorrow morning. Furthermore, strong low-level capping will be 

building over southern & central Minnesota overnight with the 12

C isotherm at 700 mb building into central Minnesota overnight.

This doesn't mean the southeast turn down Interstate 94 is 

impossible, but it does mean that the more easterly track across

northern Minnesota is probably the most-likely outcome, and/or 

the complex will begin to weaken if it moves southeastwards into

the less-favorbale environment over central & eastern 

Minnesota. As with any MCS, damaging wind gusts will be the 

primary threat should we see thunderstorms overnight, & the 

high-resolution models that do bring the complex into our area 

also depict a signal for sporadic gusty winds continuing well- 

after the line of storms. The potential timing of this 

thunderstorm complex would be early tomorrow morning (3-5 AM) 

across west-central Minnesota & tomorrow morning (5-8 AM) across

east-central MN/Twin Cities metro, by which time the 

thunderstorms would very likely be weakening (if not already 

well off to our north).



The uncertainty in the track of the thunderstorm complex also  

leads to uncertainty in the potential for excessive heat 

tomorrow. Very humid conditions are expected to develop through 

the day with swampy dew points expected to reach the mid to 

upper 70s across much of the area. The question will be how much

precipitation & cloud cover (if any) we see through the morning

that limits how warm temperatures may reach during the 

afternoon. If the most likely scenario unfolds & the 

thunderstorms stay off to our north, then temperatures will 

likely reach the upper 80s to low 90s across much of central & 

southern Minnesota & western Wisconsin. This would result in 

excessive heat conditions with heat indices around 100 degrees &

hazardous Wet Bulb Globe Temperatures through the afternoon & 

early evening. If we do get precipitation & clouds during the 

morning, then we'll likely see temperatures 5-10 degrees cooler 

across central Minnesota &western Wisconsin, but still have good

chances for excessive heat conditions across southern 

Minnesota. Decided to hold off on widespread Heat Advisories 

until late tonight when we should have a better handle on the 

track of the thunderstorm complex, but did issue a Heat Advisory

for the Twin Cities metro where our criteria is lower.



A similar scenario to tonight will also unfold overnight Friday 

into saturday morning with a severe thunderstorm complex 

expected to develop over the Dakotas during the late evening, & 

then it's just a question of where does the complex track into 

Minnesota overnight. Models generally have a consensus o this 

complex developing farther south into South Dakota, giving it 

higher chances of following the upper level winds along the 

ridge into central & southern Minnesota. We're still vague on 

the details at this timing, but our threat for damaging winds 

does appear higher across our area for this round of storms 

compared to the threat tonight.



Looking ahead: we enter a pretty typical weather pattern for  

late summer in the Upper Midwest with seasonable temperatures & 

dew points & sporadic chances for thunderstorms. Can't rule out 

some stronger storms or heavy rain but it does not appear to be 

a pattern for widespread active weather.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z SATURDAY/...

Issued at 622 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Mid-level stratocu clouds continue to spread out across the

region, though the loss of daytime heating will generally allow

the decks to rise a bit going into the evening hours, with

coverage varying between SCT and BKN. 



There is still the concern of a thunderstorm complex developing

over the Dakotas this evening and moving across northern MN

overnight through Friday morning. Chances of them diving

southeast into central MN continue to diminish, let alone

eastern MN, thus have confined PROB30 mention to just AXN-STC.

Cannot rule out TSRA entirely for other TAF sites but chances

are not high enough to warrant mention at this time. If any TSRA

do impact the sites, have kept the mention of gusty/breezy

winds. Otherwise, winds will remain generally SE with speeds in

the 5-10kt range overnight then becoming breezy/gusty during 

the day Friday.



KMSP...Chances have diminished below 20 percent of having

SHRA/TSRA move across the MSP terminal tonight so have removed

its mention from the TAF at this time. Will monitor radar trends

and adjust the TAF as needed. There is a growing chance for

precipitation Friday evening, mainly after 03z, of more

sustained rainfall and possible TSRA but will wait for model

confidence before including such mention in later TAFs.



/OUTLOOK FOR KMSP/

SAT...VFR. Chc AM MVFR/-TSRA. Wind W 5-10 kts. 

SUN...VFR. Wind SW 5-10 kts.

MON...VFR. Wind SW 5-10 kts.



&&



.MPX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

MN...Heat Advisory from noon to 10 PM CDT Friday for Anoka-Carver-

     Dakota-Hennepin-Ramsey-Scott-Washington.

WI...None.



&&



$$



DISCUSSION...ETA

AVIATION...JPC



