065

FXUS64 KMAF 072346

AFDMAF



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Midland/Odessa TX

646 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



...New AVIATION...



.KEY MESSAGES...

Updated at 643 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



- Many locations reaching into the upper 90s to low 100s through

  Sunday. Drink plenty of water and take frequent breaks in the 

  shade if working outdoors.



- Little to no rain chances (<5%) under ridging into this weekend,

  before an increased probability (10%-30%) of showers/storms over

  the western higher terrain each afternoon into evening next 

  week.



- Temperatures return to near normal early to mid next week.



&&



.SHORT TERM...

(This afternoon through Friday night)

Issued at 108 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Hazardous heat for remaining "dog days of summer" is in store into 

this weekend. Heat Risk from the 2 to 3 level range is present today 

into tomorrow as ridging strengthens into the early weekend. VIS/IR 

satellite imagery early this afternoon again shows little in the way 

of any thick cloud cover, and the mostly clear skies provided by 

large scale sinking motion of the strengthening high pressure allow 

highs from diurnal heating today and tomorrow to rise into the upper 

90s, upper 80s to lower 90s higher elevations, and triple digits 

along the Rio Grande and northeast Permian Basin. Apparent 

temperatures remain below the threshold for Heat Advisory criteria, 

even as Heat Risk increases into the major category level 3 tomorrow 

across much of Eddy County Plains, along the Rio Grande, and 

northeast Permian Basin. Dew point temperatures stay below 60 F 

courtesy of a weakening dryline located to the east of the area. 

Along with large scale sinking motion from mid to upper ridging, 

surface troughing from SE NM plains into far W TX keeps 

showers/storms west of this boundary. Therefore, even with 

daytime heating and upslope southeast winds, rain chances remain 

near zero even across far western portions of the area which will 

be east of this troughing. 



Lows tonight and tomorrow night remain above average and fall 

into the upper 60s to mid 70s F, lower to mid 60s Marfa Plateau 

and Lower Trans Pecos, mid to upper 70s F along Rio Grande 

Thursday and Friday night, as the above average daytime 

temperatures will not have time to cool to average overnight, 

despite mostly clear skies and a continued absence of boundary 

layer moisture that would enhance radiational cooling. With warmer

temperatures Friday, lows Friday night are also expected to be a 

few degrees warmer than tonight. Temperatures remain warm into the

extended, but a return to more seasonable temperatures and rain 

chances is forecast as the core of the ridging over the central 

CONUS begins to weaken/redevelop west of the area. Read the long 

term discussion for more on this.



&&



.LONG TERM...

(Saturday through Wednesday)

Issued at 108 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



The heat continues this weekend with temperatures in the upper 90s 

to low 100s as the upper-level ridge remains over the region. 

Subsident conditions resulting from the ridge should limit 

convective initiation. Ensemble guidance and cluster analysis has 

the aforementioned upper-level ridge moving to the west late Sunday 

into early next week. This will allow for temperatures to cool down 

closer to normal (96 F at Midland International Air and Space Port). 

Chances of showers/thunderstorms increase to 10-30% Monday through 

Wednesday across the western high terrain as southeasterly upslope 

flow, increased moisture, and a weaker ridge provide sufficient 

lift/instability. Overnight lows are forecast to be in the upper 60s 

to low 70s every night through the extended. 



Greening



&&



.AVIATION...

(00Z TAFS)

Issued at 643 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



VFR conditions will continue through the TAF period. S/SE winds

will generally decrease to around or below 10 knots early this

evening. S/SE winds will increase to 10-15 knots with a few gusts

above 20 knots again late Friday morning through Friday afternoon.



&&



