[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.9334","issue":"2025-08-07T23:47:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS61","cccc":"KPHI","awipsid":"AFDPHI"}]
=================================================



068

FXUS61 KPHI 072347

AFDPHI



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Mount Holly NJ

747 PM EDT Thu Aug 7 2025



.SYNOPSIS...

High pressure will remain in control through the weekend before 

gradually shifting further east into early next week. This will 

provide a prolonged stretch of mainly dry weather and relatively 

mild temperatures. A cold front will then begin to approach the area 

by the mid to late week.



&&



.NEAR TERM /THROUGH FRIDAY/...

Surface high pressure located over the Gulf of Maine will 

change very little in strength and in location through Friday. 

As a result, the onshore flow regime will continue as the area 

is nestled on the southern periphery of the high. Showers 

continue to diminish now that sunset has passed and skies should

become become mostly clear under light winds overnight. Some 

clouds may develop overnight for portions of the southern third 

of the area across southern New Jersey as well as southern 

Delaware and southern Maryland. Lows will fall into the mid to 

upper 50s for most, with low 60s around the immediate urban 

corridor and upper 60s to low 70s near the coast. 



Little change in the overall large-scale weather pattern is expected 

on Friday as the area will remain under strong influence of the high 

to the north and east. Continued onshore flow will result in another 

round of some low clouds and some breezy conditions near the coast. 

Otherwise, the surface trough to our south will break down, leading 

to a dry day across the entire area. High temps are expected to 

range from the upper 70s to mid 80s around the region.



&&



.SHORT TERM /FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY NIGHT/...

Surface high pressure to our northeast will continue to slowly 

inch further off the New England coast through Saturday. At the 

same time, a surface low will begin to take form off the coast 

of North Carolina on Friday night, butting up against our 

strong surface high. The resulting onshore flow will continue to

keep temperatures across the region below climo. 



Onshore winds will be lighter on Saturday, allowing temperatures

to bump up a few degrees. It will feel comfortable outside as 

well with a dry airmass filtering in, keeping dew points in 

check around the upper 50s to low 60s. Highs in the upper 70s to

mid 80s with lows in the mid 50s to low 60s.



&&



.LONG TERM /SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...

As our initial surface high moves out to sea, a secondary 

surface high will form nearly overhead across the Mid-Atlantic 

beneath an upper-level ridge across central Appalachia. The high

overhead will shut down the onshore flow that has kept us cool 

the past several days. As a result, Sunday looks to be a poster 

summer day in August, with mostly sunny skies, calm winds, and 

afternoon temperatures in the mid to upper 80s, right around 

climo. This surface high is slated to drift eastward early next 

week, exposing the region to the western side of this high. This

will bring gradual warming temperatures and increases in 

humidity each day through midweek. A few areas on Monday will 

reach 90 with widespread 90s by Wednesday. The increased heat 

and humidity will inevitably bring back the chance for some 

diurnal convection by Wednesday with currently 20%-40% chance 

for showers and storms in the afternoon as a cold front

approaches later in the week.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...

The following discussion is for KPHL, KPNE, KTTN, KABE, KRDG, KILG, 

KMIV, KACY and surrounding areas.



Tonight...VFR with mainly clear skies and a few clouds with bases 

around 2,500 feet for ACY and MIV overnight. Easterly winds around 3-

6 kt, becoming light and variable at times. High confidence.



Friday...VFR with a FEW/SCT clouds. Easterly winds around 7-12 kt 

with locally higher gusts near KACY. High confidence.



Outlook...



Friday night through Tuesday...VFR. No significant weather.



&&



.MARINE...

The Small Craft Advisory from offshore Little Egg Inlet to Fenwick 

Island is now in effect through 8 PM Friday. The Small Craft 

Advisory from Manasquan Inlet to Little Egg Inlet remains in effect 

until 11 PM tonight. No marine headlines are in effect elsewhere.



East-northeast winds around 10-20 kt are expected to continue 

through Friday with occasional gusts up to 25 kt possible. Seas of

4-6 feet expected. Fair weather.



Outlook...



Friday night through Tuesday...The conditions are anticipated 

to be below Small Craft Advisory criteria.



Rip Currents...



Conditions will be similar to today on Friday, with a HIGH risk for 

the development of dangerous and life threatening rip currents once 

again at the Jersey Shore and at Delaware Beaches due to onshore 

flow of 10 to 20 mph and breaking waves around 3 to 4 feet. The Rip 

Current Statement was extended to include Friday.



For Saturday, the onshore flow continues despite and winds and waves 

coming down slightly compared to Friday. However given we also have 

the Full Moon on Saturday, a HIGH risk for the development of 

dangerous and life threatening rip currents is likely. 



For specific beach forecasts, visit weather.gov/beach/phi



&&



.PHI WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

PA...None.

NJ...High Risk for Rip Currents through Friday evening for NJZ014-

     024>026.

DE...High Risk for Rip Currents through Friday evening for DEZ004.

MD...None.

MARINE...Small Craft Advisory until 11 PM EDT this evening for ANZ451.

     Small Craft Advisory until 8 PM EDT Friday for ANZ452>455.



&&



$$



SYNOPSIS...DeSilva/MJL

NEAR TERM...DeSilva/Robertson

SHORT TERM...MJL

LONG TERM...MJL

AVIATION...Cooper/DeSilva/MJL/Robertson

MARINE...Cooper/DeSilva/Fitzsimmons/MJL



