110

FXUS64 KMRX 072350

AFDMRX



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Morristown TN

750 PM EDT Thu Aug 7 2025



...New AVIATION...



.DISCUSSION...

Issued at 1232 PM EDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Key Messages: 



1. A slight warming trend into next week with temperatures 

trending to around seasonal normals.



2. Isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms expected each 

afternoon through the weekend, especially across the terrain, then

rain chances will edge higher next week.



Discussion:



We start the period with weak upper troughing over the area with 

upper ridging to our west, and strong surface high pressure to our 

northeast and east. The weak trough will give way to upper level  

ridging Friday into the weekend, while the surface high to our 

northeast and east will continue to hinder a quick warm up by 

feeding cooler Atlantic air from the northeast and east into our 

area. We will see less coverage of showers and storms Friday 

through Sunday, with mainly isolated to scattered convection 

especially over the higher terrain areas during the afternoons. 

Temperatures will edge up slightly by the weekend, but only to 

near seasonal normals. 



Models generally agree that moisture will increase early in the week 

as low level flow turns more southerly, with an increase in 

scattered mainly afternoon showers and thunderstorms for the Monday 

through Thursday period. A broad trough moving through the Great 

Lakes region may tamp down the ridge briefly in the 

Tuesday/Wednesday time frame, although the trend of the models is 

for this to be less impactful than it looked at this time

yesterday, and its associated cold front will likely stay well to

our north. Temperatures by Thursday will likely be back to 

slightly above seasonal normals. 



&&



.AVIATION...

(00Z TAFS)

Issued at 741 PM EDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Convection will continue to dwindle into the late PM hours with predominant

VFR conditions expected through the TAF cycle. Expect some low- 

level clouds to develop during the Fri AM hours but HREF 

probabilities of MVFR cigs are 30% or less so have kept it as a 

sct deck for the time being. Coverage of convection Friday 

afternoon looks to be too low to warrant any mention at this time,

but may be needed in future TAF issuances. 





