137

FXUS65 KFGZ 072351

AFDFGZ



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Flagstaff AZ

451 PM MST Thu Aug 7 2025



.SYNOPSIS...High pressure will continue to dominate the weather

through early next week. Look for above normal temperatures and 

mostly dry conditions. Isolated showers and thunderstorms are

forecast to continue through the weekend. Outside of limited

shower activity, expect near-critical to critical fire weather 

conditions through Friday, especially across northwest Coconino 

County.



&&



.DISCUSSION...Strong high pressure aloft will remain centered over

New Mexico/Arizona with troughing across much of California, the 

Great Basin and Pacific Northwest through Friday. As a result, 

the pressure gradient between these two systems will produce 

breezy daytime winds from the west to southwest remainder of today

and on Friday. The strongest winds will be along and north of a 

Prescott- Flagstaff-Four Corners line where sustained winds of 10 

to 20 mph gusting to 25 to 35 mph are forecast. South of this line

winds will be generally 5 to 15 mph gusting to 20 mph. The air 

mass will remain on the dry side with only slight chances (15% or 

lower) for afternoon and evening showers and thunderstorms for 

most of Arizona. The only exception is the far eastern Mogollon 

Rim and White Mountains areas where showers and storms will be 

more scattered (30% coverage). Hot daytime temperatures will 

continue with an Extreme Heat Warning for the lower elevations of 

the Grand Canyon and Yavapai County. The dry conditions will 

combine with gusty winds to produce an elevated fire threat 

through Friday. In fact, a Red Flag Warning has been issued for 

the Kaibab Plateau for Friday where a few wind gusts could 

approach 40 mph. Not a good outlook at all for those fighting 

fires across the state. 



For Saturday and Sunday...The high center will shift westward

across southern Arizona. As high center moves across southern 

Arizona there will be a slight push of moisture from over the 

Gulf of California. This push of moisture will slightly increase 

the chances for showers thunderstorms primarily along and south of

a Prescott-Flagstaff-Four Corners line. As the high position 

shifts westward pressure gradients will weaken substantially 

resulting in lighter and more typical winds at 10 to 20 mph from 

the west to southwest. The high will weaken with slightly cooler 

daytime temperatures the result. Don't get too excited, those 

temperatures will remain above normal.



From Monday onward...The high center becomes established to the 

northwest of Arizona for a drying northwesterly flow on Monday 

and Tuesday. For Wednesday through the end of next week it appears

another trough will approach Arizona. The question is, will the 

trough be oriented in such a way to produce southerly flow and 

pull moisture northward from over the Gulf of California or will 

the trough produce a drier southwest flow? The way our luck has 

been running, probably the latter scenario.



&&



.AVIATION...Friday 08/00Z through Saturday 09/00Z...VFR conditions. 

Localized FU/HZ downwind from wildfire activity, especially along

a line north of KGCN to KPGA. Isolated -SHRA/-TSRA through 03Z

this evening and again after 18Z Fri, primarily in Navajo and 

Apache counties. Winds SW-W 10-20 kts gusting to 25-30 kts 

through 03Z this evening, light and variable overnight, then SW-W 

10-20 kts with gusts 20-35 kts - strongest north of I-40 and west

of Hwy 89.



OUTLOOK...Saturday 09/00Z through Monday 11/00Z...VFR conditions. 

Localized FU/HZ downwind from wildfire activity, especially along

a line north of KGCN to KPGA. Isolated to scattered -SHRA/-TSRA

each day, primarily from KFLG eastward. Daytime winds SW-W 10-15 

kts gusting to 25 kts. Winds light and variable during the 

overnight periods.



&&



.FIRE WEATHER...Friday and Saturday...Hot, breezy, and mostly dry. A 

Red Flag Warning has been issued for the Kaibab Plateau on Friday, 

where critical fire weather is expected. Isolated to scattered 

showers/storms primarily along the higher terrain east of Flagstaff 

into Apache and Navajo counties. Gusty/erratic winds and cloud-to-

ground are the main hazards near storms. Winds are west through 

southwest 10-20 mph gusts 20-30 mph Friday and Saturday, but gusts 

over 35 mph expected in the Kaibab Plateau on Friday. Minimum RH 

will be 10-20% each afternoon.  



Sunday through Tuesday...Not as hot with lighter winds. Isolated to 

scattered storms primarily along the higher terrain east of 

Flagstaff. The main hazards near storms will be cloud-to-ground 

lightning and gusty/erratic outflow winds. Winds are west 5-15 mph 

gusting to 20 mph. The Minimum RH is generally 10-25% on Sunday, 

then 6-20% Monday and Tuesday.



&&



.FGZ WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

Red Flag Warning from 11 AM to 8 PM MST Friday for AZZ104.



Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM MST Tuesday for AZZ006.



Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM MST Friday for AZZ018-037.



&&



$$



PUBLIC...McCollum

AVIATION...JJ

FIRE WEATHER...Lewandowski



For Northern Arizona weather information visit

weather.gov/flagstaff



