[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.9413","issue":"2025-08-07T23:52:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS65","cccc":"KABQ","awipsid":"AFDABQ"}]
=================================================



148

FXUS65 KABQ 072352 AAA

AFDABQ



Area Forecast Discussion...UPDATED

National Weather Service Albuquerque NM

552 PM MDT Thu Aug 7 2025



...New AVIATION...



.KEY MESSAGES...

Issued at 1209 AM MDT Thurs Aug 7 2025

 

- There is high confidence in widespread moderate to major heat

  risk impacts through tomorrow afternoon, which may cause health

  issues for individuals without adequate cooling and hydration. 

  Some areas will approach record highs.



- Gusty and erratic winds across western and central NM through 

  Friday from isolated to widely scattered virga showers and dry 

  thunderstorms.



- Temperatures not as hot along with better shower and storm

  coverage later this weekend into early next week.



- Marginal Risk of severe weather for northeastern New Mexico on

  Saturday afternoon and early evening.



&&



.SHORT TERM...

(This evening through Friday night)

Issued at 246 PM MDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Today's 18z upper air sounding indicates that the high pressure over 

New Mexico is still holding steady, with a near record 500 mb height 

of 600 dm. As a result, temperatures this afternoon are forecast to 

be 5 to 12 degrees above average for early August. The Albuquerque 

metro is once again forecast to break the century mark, but similar 

to the last several days, mid level cloud development and virga 

showers may once again prevent us tying the daily record high of 

100. The 18z upper sounding also recorded a slightly higher PWAT 

than the last couple of days at 0.84 inches. This may be an 

indication that there is a little bit more moisture for the atmosphere 

to work with, and current satellite imagery is showing rapid 

development of scattered to broken mid level cloud cover over. 

Conditions at the surface remain quite dry (dewpoints of 35 to 40 

degrees), so wetting precipitation looks to be minimal and isolated 

to the high terrain where most storms will develop, but it wouldn't 

be surprising for some areas to receive a couple of tenths of 

localized precipitation. However, with such dry lower levels, 

inverted-v soundings would indicate that gusty and erratic winds 

from virga bombs will be the main concern. With DCAPE between 1000 

and 1500 J/kg, strong gusts of 50 to 55 mph may be possible. These 

virga showers will diminish around sunset, but mid and high level 

cloud cover looks to remain throughout the night.



The weather pattern on Friday looks to be pretty similar to today's, 

with hot temperatures throughout the Land of Enchantment and virga 

showers and dry thunderstorms mainly for western areas. A trough 

will begin to dig south over Alberta and Saskatchewan and eventually 

into the northern American Rockies. This will flatten and stretch 

the upper level high over New Mexico. While 500 mb heights look to 

drop into the 594 to 596 dm range, it is still quite anomalously 

strong for this time of year, so high temperatures will once again 

be 5 to 12 degrees above average. Heat advisories will be in effect 

for the Middle Rio Grande Valley and Chaves plains, including the 

Albuquerque and Roswell metro areas. Aside from the threat of gusty 

virga showers over the high terrain and nearby lower elevation 

areas, some more robust storms and could develop in northeast New 

Mexico. An upper level disturbance associated with the trough over 

Canada may be able to invigorate storms enough to drop a few tenths 

of wetting rain, particularly in northeast areas where dewpoints 

will be higher.



&&



.LONG TERM...

(Saturday through Wednesday)

Issued at 246 PM MDT Thu Aug 7 2025



The trough over Canada will deepen into an upper low over Manitoba 

and keep lower pressure heights over the central CONUS through the 

weekend. The monsoon high over New Mexico continually weakens until 

pressure heights plateau at around 590-592 dm, binging temperatures 

back to near normal for this time of year. As the low spins over 

Canada, a few disturbances and lobes of vorticity will swing through 

the central Rockies and Great Plains. This forcing will help 

generate some stronger thunderstorms, particularly in the northeast 

plains where there will be greater shear for storms to possibly 

become severe. Elsewhere, with PWATs remaining near or below 

average, wetting precipitation looks to be light and isolated to the 

high terrain, but chances for showers will be higher than the last 

several days and as a result burn scar flash flooding will be more 

of a concern. For Sunday, some guidance begins to split on shower 

coverage. Ridging looks to remain over the Great Basin, but the 

eastern extent of its influence is uncertain and it may be able to 

inhibit shower development during the afternoon. A shortwave trough 

looks to swing through northeastern areas on Sunday evening, 

bringing increased chances of showers and pushing a moist backdoor 

front through the northeast plains. The GFS solution brings in 

deeper troughing into New Mexico and is much more bullish on shower 

coverage and rain amounts. However, most ensembles are in better 

agreement of a nose of high pressure pushing into western NM and 

allowing for some subsidence to hinder storm development along 

western and central areas. 



Heading into next week, the monsoon high looks to build and shift 

back over New Mexico by midweek. Monsoonal moisture will be back on 

the rise, particularly towards the end of the week as the high 

continues to push east into Texas. This will allow a corridor of 

moisture from the Pacific to once again stream up towards New 

Mexico, increasing chances for burn scar flash flooding.



&&



.AVIATION...

(00Z TAFS)

Issued at 536 PM MDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Virga showers and dry storms will continue to decrease in coverage

through the evening, but gusty outflow winds to 40KT will remain a

possibility at all terminals through around 03Z. Light winds will

prevail around the region tonight, except for the eastern plains

where a S/SW breeze will hang on through the night. There is a low

chance of LLWS in the eastern plains at KTCC tonight between 06Z

and 12Z.



Tomorrow afternoon will be very similar to today with scattered 

showers and dry storms favoring the high terrain. Coverage will be

greatest south of I-40 and there is a low chance that storms 

develop into a semi-organized line in the eastern plains during 

the late afternoon. Gusty and erratic outflow winds will remain 

the main aviation concern through the TAF period.



&&



.FIRE WEATHER...

Issued at 246 PM MDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Anomalously strong high pressure over New Mexico 

will continue to keep hotter and drier conditions through Friday. 

Virga showers and dry thunderstorms may create a rogue lightning 

strike and some erratic gusty winds. Late Friday and heading into 

the weekend, a trough digging into the northern Rockies looks to 

bring some stronger westerly flow into the region particularly near 

the Colorado border. As a result, elevated to locally critical fire 

weather conditions may arise in the Four Corners area where relative 

humidity will dip into the single digits and winds speeds will be 

close to 20 kts. Shower coverage increases heading into the middle 

of next week a more traditional monsoon plume looks to return over 

the region.



&&



