187

FXUS63 KEAX 072354

AFDEAX



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Kansas City/Pleasant Hill MO

654 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Hot and humid conditions should continue through at least

  Saturday.



- Hottest day expected to be tomorrow, with afternoon heat 

  indices ranging from 100 to 108 degrees.

    - Heat advisory in effect region wide 1 pm through 8 pm

      tomorrow.



- Unsettled weather with multiple chances for showers and

  thunderstorms arrives for Saturday night through early Monday

  morning.

   - A few strong to severe storms will be possible.

   - Locally moderate to heavy rainfall and flooding may be 

     possible.



&&



.DISCUSSION...

Issued at 300 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



The MCS which initially developed over central Iowa around 3 AM

this morning and then slowly drifted southward into northern

Missouri has finally largely decayed and exited the region to

the east. However, with rain-cooled air and some cloud cover

lingering, high temperatures for areas impacted by the MCS have

been revised down by quite a bit (3 pm temp at Kirksville is

only 78). Elsewhere, the heat and humidity has been generally as

advertised with 3 pm temperatures ranging from the upper 80s to

lower 90s, as the 599 dam H5 high centered over the NM/AZ 

border continues to heavily influence our current weather. Warm

temperatures continue into tonight, with most locations only

dropping into the mid to upper 70s. Unlike the previous 2

overnights, there is virtually zero chance for an MCS overnight

tonight into early Friday morning. 



As we head into tomorrow, a mid/upper trough is progged to move

across the Intermountain West and into the Northern High Plains

with a surface low developing across the Dakotas. Meanwhile, the

stout H5 high remains centered over SW New Mexico, but the ridge 

becomes more elongated east to west, allowing for some of the

higher temperatures more toward the Kansas and Oklahoma Plains

to make their way eastward into our CWA. Temperatures are 

forecast to climb into the mid to upper 90s for most locations,

with dew points in the lower to perhaps even mid 70s. And with

essentially zero chances for precipitation and mostly clear

skies expected, there does not appear to be much of a chance for

something to derail the heat. Thus, a heat advisory has been

issued CWA wide from 1 pm to 8 pm tomorrow for heat indices 

ranging from 100 to 108 degrees. 



By late tomorrow night, the aforementioned mid/upper trough

takes on a negative tilt as it moves into the Dakotas, with a 

deepening closed H5 low developing near the southern 

Saskatchewan and Manitoba border. This should send a cold front 

extending from Minnesota south southwestward into far SW Iowa 

and SE Nebraska, stalling out essentially on the doorstep of 

far NW Missouri by early Saturday morning. This will allow for 

some slight chance (20%) PoPs across NW Missouri for Saturday 

morning and afternoon. Hot and humid conditions should continue

through Saturday afternoon for most locations (south of the

increased cloud cover and chances for rain toward the stalled

cold front). Temperatures may approach the upper 90s toward the

Ozark Plateau, and the heat advisory may need to be extended

through Saturday evening for those locations. Guidance suggests

a subtle shortwave trough with enhanced (50+ knot) mid level 

westerly winds moving from Colorado into SE Nebraska by late 

Saturday night, which may trigger showers and thunderstorms 

across the region by as early as late Saturday evening and 

continuing into Sunday morning/afternoon. A few storms could be

strong to marginally severe, and SPC has indeed highlighted

areas along and north of Interstate 70 within a marginal risk

for severe storms. With PWATs on the order of 1.7 to 1.9", 

locally moderate rainfall will also be possible, and WPC has a

very similar area (along and north of I-70) highlighted within a

marginal risk for excessive rainfall through 7 AM Sunday. Chance

to likely (40% to 60%) PoPs continue into Sunday afternoon, but

better chances for more widespread showers and thunderstorms

with a higher threat for locally moderate to heavy rainfall

arrives Sunday evening into late Sunday night/early Monday

morning as we get another wave of mid to upper level dynamic 

support to interact with the stalled front (which should begin 

to make some very slight southeastward progress by Sunday night)

and PWATs increasing a bit closer to 2". Accordingly, WPC has

upgraded areas along and north of I-70 to a slight risk for

excessive rainfall through 7 AM Monday. 



Rain chances should linger through Monday and even Tuesday

morning as the front remains stalled across the CWA, but it

should eventually undergo frontolysis and eventually completely

dissolve. High temperatures for Monday through Wednesday should

be close to seasonal normals.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z SATURDAY/...

Issued at 653 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



VFR conditions are expected to prevail through the period. Gusty

south winds will persist for the first few hours, relaxing

slightly overnight. Gusts through 02 to 03Z will near 25 kts.

South winds will increase in the morning, post-sunrsie, with

gusts around 25 kts at times. 



&&



.EAX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

MO...Heat Advisory from 1 PM to 8 PM CDT Friday for MOZ001>008-

     011>017-020>025-028>033-037>040-043>046-053-054.

KS...Heat Advisory from 1 PM to 8 PM CDT Friday for KSZ025-057-060-

     102>105.



&&



$$



DISCUSSION...Williams

AVIATION...Kurtz



