[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.9475","issue":"2025-08-07T23:56:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS63","cccc":"KBIS","awipsid":"AFDBIS"}]
=================================================



210

FXUS63 KBIS 072356

AFDBIS



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Bismarck ND

656 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Significant severe weather is possible through tonight, with 

  very large hail up to the size of baseballs, damaging winds as

  high as 80 mph, and a few tornadoes.



- The risk for scattered severe storms shifts into far eastern

  North Dakota on Friday, with isolated severe storms still 

  possible over most of western and central North Dakota.

 

- Temperatures cool on Friday and through the weekend with 70s 

  and lower 80s expected through the weekend.



- The threat for severe weather will remain low through the

  weekend and into early next week.



&&



.UPDATE...

Issued at 656 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Severe weather has not progressed quite as expected to start the

evening. There has been just enough of a cap across the majority

of western and central North Dakota to prevent convection,

although for a few hours now we have seen cumulus trying to

grow, with numerous orphan anvils observed on satellite. Current

main area of development is in the northwest part of the state,

where mesoanalysis advertises around 45 knots of deep layer

shear and ~1300 J/kg of instability, although thinking the storm

is still a little elevated. Some signs of expansion to the

southwest, back into Montana. Thinking there is a low (20

percent chance) of an isolated supercell or two across central

North Dakota outside of any activity across the north, before

attention turns to the convection ongoing further upstream

across Montana. One area of storms is south of Glasgow and an

additional one is in the Billings area. If these progress as

forecast, the expectation is storms would become more linear in

nature and speed up as they approach the state line. With no

storms over most of the forecast area today, the environment

remains highly supportive of severe weather, potentially

significant, with wind gusts up to 80 mph the primary hazard. 



&&



.DISCUSSION...

Issued at 129 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Latest satellite imagery is showing a shortwave tracking from

northeast Montana into northwest North Dakota. Another strong

shortwave is tracking eastward across southwest Montana. Latest

radar shows the morning convection has for the most part, moved

north into Canada. A short segment of likely elevated is 

located over far northeast Montana. The latest surface analysis 

shows a surface low in the Mondak region with a warm front 

extending northeastward from the low, into northeast ND. The 

area north of the surface low, around west central ND looks to 

be an area of significant low level convergence. Latest 

satellite also indicates approaching cirrus which implies 

increased ascent, and increased agitation in the cu field in the

McKenzie, Dunn, Golden Valley and Billings county area. It's 

looking more and more like this could be the area of initial CI 

in our area.



Needless to say, nothing has changed in the ingredients area.

Still very unstable air with strong deep layer shear. Not going

to repeat this again, except to say that all of our earlier

significant threats remain in the table (Baseball sized hail, 

80 mph winds and a few tornados possible). 



A major concern is the storm mode and the storm mode evolution 

today and tonight. Short term models have been inconsistent. As 

mentioned above, in our area, the west central looks like the 

preferred area of CI. Cams seem to favor, and WOFS also hints at

a potential supercell that tracks east from here, remaining 

north of the I-94 corridor. This would favor very large hail and

a possible tornadic supercell. Ingredients are there for that 

scenario. 



Another possible scenario would be quicker upscale growth from

initial CI and a linear complex developing. This scenario has

shown two main tendencies. One would be more of a due east 

track of a complex, which would leave the more pristine 

atmosphere in southern ND, untouched, and primed for a second 

round of convection eminating from Montana. Although there is

some indication of more capping along the South Dakota border.



A second scenario from a quicker upscale growth perspective 

would be a more southeast progression as the cold pool pushes 

southward, this may take the complex south of the I-94 corridor,

and more importantly, could alter the highly unstable airmass

over southern ND, possibly limiting the potential for a complex

entering from Montana later this evening, or overnight. Cams 

earlier today were showing the detriment of a storm complex from

Montana as it progresses through western ND. 



If the initial development remains mainly discrete, as 

mentioned above, this could alter the pristine environment over 

southern ND somewhat, but we think it would leave enough 

instability that the later convection entering from Montana 

would have enough fuel to continue it's track across North 

Dakota, at least western and central ND. 



Of course, what eventually happens will probably be something

else, or a combination of these scenarios. We will continue to

update Social Media content through the event so continue to

monitor for our latest thinking of how this event will

eventually unfold. 



The potential for strong to severe convection remains on Friday,

but there has been a pretty significant change. The Enhanced

risk, that extended as far west as the James River Valley has

been downgraded. There is a slight risk now in this area, with a

marginal risk for severe storms over much of western and central

ND. There will likely be some strong to severe convection in

western and central ND, but the potential for a more organized

event has diminished over our area for the time being. 

Atmosphere may be pretty well worked over in our area with 

convection behind a surface boundary. We'll see how things 

evolve after today and tonight. 



Once we see an end of the convection on Friday it does look like

we will see a break in the severe convection, at least through

the weekend and possibly into early next week. Temperatures cool

into mainly the 70s through the weekend before climbing again

early next week.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z SATURDAY/...

Issued at 656 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Convection will be moving through the forecast area through the

first half of the TAF period, likely impacting much of western 

and central ND. It's possible there could be more than one round

of thunderstorms but for now kept things simple with just one 

period of Thunderstorms at each site. With any storms 

tonight, some will be severe, with very large hail and 

destructive winds possible. Any storm could also produce IFR to 

LIFR visibility from torrential rainfall. Late tonight into 

Friday morning there is a pretty good signal for quite a bit of

low stratus/fog across the forecast area. Some improvement in

ceilings is expected Friday afternoon.



&&



.BIS WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

None.



&&



$$



UPDATE...Jones

DISCUSSION...TWH

AVIATION...Jones



