[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.9488","issue":"2025-08-07T23:57:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS63","cccc":"KLBF","awipsid":"AFDLBF"}]
=================================================



223

FXUS63 KLBF 072357

AFDLBF



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service North Platte NE

657 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Widely scattered thunderstorms are expected this evening.

  Gusty winds would be a concern with any storm. 



- At least scattered showers and thunderstorms appear possible

  behind a cold front Friday night. 



- Cooler and drier conditions for the weekend as a cold front

  settles and stalls just to our south. 



- Warmer again next week with isolated thunderstorms possible.



&&



.SHORT TERM /THROUGH FRIDAY NIGHT/...

Issued at 325 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Widely scattered thunderstorms are expected to develop in a well 

mixed boundary layer near a dry line late this afternoon. Initiation 

will be west of Highway 61. Boundary layer moisture is not deep west 

of a North Platte to Valentine line, and any storm that develops 

will be high based and pose a gusty wind threat. Will also have to 

watch for any dry lightning strikes across the eastern Panhandle, 

where grasses are starting to cure. Overall shear is weak, and as 

storms track east along and north of I-80 this evening gusty winds 

will remain a concern until storms diminish mid-evening. 



A strong shortwave will track from the northern Rockies into the 

Dakotas and southern Canada Friday. This will drive a strong cold 

front southward through the area during the day Friday. Winds will 

tend to turn west/southwest just ahead of the front with dew points 

mixing out somewhat just ahead of the front. The limited instability 

and strong capping should keep anything from developing along the 

front as it passes. The airmass behind the front will be moist for a 

time Friday night. Storms that develop in the higher terrain will 

move eastward though this post-frontal moisture Friday night. Shear 

does increase as stronger winds aloft increase, but the overall 

severe threat looks to be limited. Otherwise another hot day ahead 

of the front, with highs approaching 100 degrees. Humidity remains 

higher across eastern portions of north central Nebraska, so the 

heat index will be an issue in this area. Have issued a heat 

advisory from eastern portions of north central Nebraska through 

central and into southwest Nebraska ahead of the cold front. 



Surface high pressure builds into the area Saturday behind the 

front. Sinking air and drier advection from the northwest will 

significantly lower dew points over what has been observed the last 

several days. Temperatures will also be cooler, with highs in the 

80s. Should end up being a very pleasant day with the lower 

humidity.



&&



.LONG TERM /SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...

Issued at 325 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



The cold front driven southward by the northern plains upper 

low/shortwave, will stall across northern Kansas Sunday and Monday. 

It appears the better chance for rainfall will remain across 

southern Nebraska and especially into northern Kansas during this 

time.  



Upper level high pressure will begin to expand northward across the 

desert southwest Tuesday and beyond. This will bring a return to 

near average highs in the upper 80s to around 90 degrees. Weak 

disturbances rotating around the northern periphery of the high 

could spark a few isolated thunderstorms Tuesday through the end of 

the week.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z FRIDAY/...

Issued at 657 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



An isolated thunderstorm west of the KVTN terminal may reach

KVTN around 0030Z, an have included a TEMPO VRB20G30KT 5SM 

-tsra BKN080CB from 00Z-02Z. Otherwise, VFR conditions are 

expected at the KVTN and KLBF terminals the next 24 hours. Winds

will remain gusty up to 20KT at KVTN this evening, and again 

after 17Z, as a cold front moves through the area. Winds at KLBF

will main remain below 15KT tonight through Friday afternoon, 

even as a cold front passes through around 20Z. 



&&



.LBF WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

Heat Advisory until 8 PM CDT /7 PM MDT/ this evening for 

NEZ005>010-025>029-036>038-057>059-069>071.

Heat Advisory from 1 PM CDT /noon MDT/ to 8 PM CDT /7 PM MDT/ 

Friday for NEZ007-010-027>029-038-058-059-069>071.



&&



$$



UPDATE...NMJ

SHORT TERM...Taylor

LONG TERM...Taylor

AVIATION...Roberg



