[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.9654","issue":"2025-08-07T23:59:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS61","cccc":"KBTV","awipsid":"AFDBTV"}]
=================================================



390

FXUS61 KBTV 072359

AFDBTV



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Burlington VT

759 PM EDT Thu Aug 7 2025



.SYNOPSIS...

Scattered showers and thunderstorms mainly across the higher terrain 

will dissipate overnight with areas of patchy fog likely. 

Temperatures will cool back into the mid 50s to mid 60s tonight, 

before warming into the upper 70s to mid 80s on Friday. A few 

additional showers and storms are possible on Friday afternoon, 

followed by an extended period of much above normal temperatures for 

Sunday through most of next week.



&&



.NEAR TERM /THROUGH FRIDAY/...

As of 208 PM EDT Thursday...Any trrn or lake breeze showers/storms wl

slowly dissipate after sunset with the loss of sfc

heating/instability. Did note the latest 16z HRRR shows lingering

instability with activity persisting thru 02z, before weakening. Did

extend some chc pops along the eastern Dacks and parts of central VT

mtns thru 02z to account for this potential. Storm motions have only

been 3 to 5 knots, resulting in localized heavy down pours, but

areal footprint of qpf is rather small. Sfc obs indicate a few spots

have reached 1.0 or so, and we will continue to watch hydro closely

thru this evening. Otherwise, did note dwpts are 3 to 5 degrees

warmer than 24 hrs ago and cumulus field is greater this aftn, so

this combined with light winds and clearing skies, feel fog wl be

more widespread tonight. Greatest probability wl be areas that

received rainfall today, along with valleys of the NEK. Temps very

similar to last night with values ranging from the mid 50s to mid

60s for overnight lows.



For Friday very limited change is anticipated with regards to

pops/temps and winds from today. Showers/storms wl develop acrs the

trrn/lake breeze boundary again with similar thermodynamics per

sounding data. Sfc based CAPE values in the 1000-1500 J/kg range, pw

values btwn 1.4" and 1.6", and very weak flow thru 400mb. the NAM

12z sounding at EFK shows <10 knots thru 300mb with storm motions of

4 to 6 knots at best. Depth of moisture and instability is slightly

less on Friday, so expect areal coverage of showers/storms to be a

little less. Highest pops wl be eastern Dacks into parts of the NEK,

with less pops over the SLV and CPV. Progged 925mb temps are 1 to 2

degrees warmer on Friday and we are already 86F here at BTV today,

so thinking upper 70s to m/u 80s.



&&



.SHORT TERM /FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY NIGHT/...

As of 208 PM EDT Thursday...Mid/upper lvl ridge builds acrs the

central Great Lakes into the NE CONUS with dry conditions and

warming temps. Given the recent dry weather and relatively

comfortable dwpts feel temps could over achieve again on Saturday,

based on temp trends the past couple of days. Progged 925mb temps

warm a degree or two with values in the 22- 24C range, combined with

developing sw downsloping flow, feel a few spot lower 90s are likely

in the CPV on Sat aftn, away from the lake breeze. Soundings are

pretty dry in the 850 to 500mb layer, but weak s/w energy tries to

ride over the ridge late Sat and could fire a shower or two over

northern NY. Probability of this is <20% attm. Areas of valley fog

are likely overnight on Friday and Saturday with temps mid 50s to

mid 60s.



&&



.LONG TERM /SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...

As of 208 PM EDT Thursday...The main concern during this time frame

will be the period of hot weather Sunday into early next week, with

Monday and Tuesday looking to be the hottest days. High temperatures

in the broad valleys look to climb into the low to mid 90s, with

dewpoint temperatures slightly increasing into the low to mid 60s.

Overnight lows will be on the mild side, only dropping into the 60s

and low 70s which will provide limited relief from the daytime heat.

Heat index values may be close to Heat Advisory criteria, but may

fall just short depending on dewpoints and any cloud cover but

trends will need to be monitored as we get closer. Even if values

don't reach criteria, it will still be a stretch of hot weather so

be sure to take proper precautions to stay safe and remember to stay

hydrated.



Towards the end of the forecast period, a cold front will try to

move across the region, providing some chances for precipitation.

There is still a large amount of uncertainty regarding this feature,

in both the strength of the front itself and the overall timing.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...

Through 00Z Saturday...Patchy fog will develop across the region 

tonight. It should form this evening at SLK due to rainfall today. 

It will likely develop at MPV sometime after 06Z and there is the 

chance it develops at EFK later tonight, but confidence is still 

pretty low at this point. Any fog lifts soon after sunrise and VFR 

conditions should prevail into the early afternoon. Winds will 

generally be under 10 KTs, mostly light or terrain driven tonight 

and southerly tomorrow.



Outlook...



Friday Night: VFR. NO SIG WX.

Saturday: VFR. NO SIG WX.

Saturday Night: VFR. NO SIG WX.

Sunday: VFR. NO SIG WX.

Sunday Night: VFR. NO SIG WX.

Monday: VFR. NO SIG WX.

Monday Night: VFR. NO SIG WX.

Tuesday: VFR. NO SIG WX.



&&



.BTV WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

VT...None.

NY...None.



&&



$$

