393

FXUS63 KARX 072359

AFDARX



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service La Crosse WI

659 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Chance (20-40%) for showers and storms along and south of I-90

  from this afternoon through tonight. A strong storm or two

  with gusty winds and small hail cannot be ruled out.



- Warm and humid for Friday with highs reaching into the lower

  90s and heat indices from the middle 90s to near 100 for most.



- Increasing shower and storm chances (40-75%) for Saturday afternoon

  and evening. There is the potential for some storms to be 

  strong to severe with damaging wind gusts and large hail.



&&



.DISCUSSION...

Issued at 158 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Today: Shower & Storm Chances Later This Afternoon into Tonight 



The overall synoptic pattern can be noted on GOES-19 water vapor 

imagery and 07.15z RAP 500mb heights with a fairly pronounced 

convective complex moving through southern Iowa and northern 

Missouri. Upper-level ridging can be noted further west across the 

Plains. As we continue through the remainder of the afternoon and 

into the evening/overnight, the main feature we will have to watch 

will be how an axis of 850mb moisture transport and a very subtle 

mid-level shortwave manifest itself over the coming hours. 

Currently, the recent CAMs have backed off considerably on any 

convection across the region for later today. However, given the 

current RAP/HRRR soundings keep instability profiles uncapped for 

the late afternoon and evening hours at La Crosse and across much of 

southwest WI. As a result, have maintained some shower and storm 

chances (20-40%) along and south of I-90 for this afternoon through 

the evening in spite of the dry CAM solutions. Given the lack of 0-

6km shear, thinking that any severe potential is fairly limited but 

with decent MLCAPE to around 1500-2000 J/kg and respectable mid-

level lapse rates to around 7 C/km, cannot rule out some small hail 

or gusty winds in any stronger storms. 



Friday: Warmer & Humid



As we head into Friday, some briefing upper-level ridging pushes 

into the region which will provide ample subsidence to clear our 

skies out for much of the daytime hours on Friday. Consequently, 

would expect some amplification to daytime heating which in turn is 

reflected in the inter-quartile NBM percentile data which keeps 

highs ranging between the middle 80s to lower 90s. Additionally with 

dewpoints in the 70s, expecting heat indices to range from the 

middle 90s to some locations topping 100 degrees. 



This Weekend: Potential for Strong to Severe Storms Saturday



For the weekend, the main feature of note will be a larger synoptic 

trough that entrenches itself in the Northern Plains for Saturday. 

As it slowly meanders eastward, a surface cold front will progress 

southeastward towards the local area during the day. Still some

difference between deterministic models (GFS/NAM) as to the 

exact location of the boundary with the GFS favoring the 

boundary on our doorstep as early as late morning and the NAM 

favoring a late afternoon solution. This will likely be a key 

point of uncertainty over the coming days that may not be fully 

understood until upstream convection is better resolved. 

Additionally, noticing in NAM/GFS soundings a large degree of 

capping, particularly further west of the Mississippi River 

associated with an elevated mixed layer, which may suppress 

convection until later afternon. As far as the overall 

environment, instability profiles look pretty impressive with 

high probabilities (50-80%) in the GEFS & Canadian ensemble for 

SBCAPE values over 2500 J/kg, east of where the EML is present. 

So certainly looks like an environment where storms will develop

quickly once convective initiation occurs. Shear profile may 

potentially be favorable as well depending on where the mid- 

level wind field associated with the broader trough sets up. 

However, the current NAM/GFS solutions tend to keep much of the 

shear both along and behind the boundary which would limit storm

organization to being on the front. Primary threat with any 

severe storms in this setup would be likely damaging winds given

the 25th percentile DCAPE being over 900 J/kg at La Crosse in 

the grand ensemble (GEFS/EC/Canadian ensemble) along with any 

cold pool organization. Large hail will be possible as well 

within more organized cores given the high instability, 

conditional bulk shear and mid-level lapse rates of 7-7.5 C/km. 



Sunday will likely observe the aforementioned cold front just south 

of the local area which will once again act as a focus for showers 

and storms to develop on. However, the exact position of this 

boundary will be dictated by how the previous days convection 

behaves. Regardless, the grand ensemble (GEFS/EC/Canadian 

ensemble) have very high probabilities (60-100%) for measurable 

precipitation south of I-90 through Sunday evening so seems 

fairly likely that showers and storms will be around. With 

robust MLCAPE of 2000-3000 J/kg in the 07.12z GFS south of the 

front, some stronger storms may pop-up with gusty winds possible

considering DCAPE values in excess of 900 J/kg. 



Early Next Week: Seasonable with Lingering Storm Chances 



By early next week, the aforementioned synoptic trough continues to 

slowly pivot eastward subjecting our area to quasi-zonal flow and 

the potential for pieces of shortwave energy to pass through the 

area. Additionally, the aforementioned boundary will likely linger 

across portions of the region, acting as an additional focus for 

convection to develop. Consequently, the NBM holds onto 

precipitation chances into early next week due to these potential 

features. With zonal flow aloft, expecting little modification to 

the ambient airmass so noticing high temperatures remaining 

seasonable to start the new week in guidance.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z SATURDAY/...

Issued at 632 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Possible aviation impacts for 08.00Z TAF issuance overnight 

through Friday morning. Similar to yesterday (07.12Z), low level

moisture may build an MVFR-IFR cloud deck and/or IFR-LIFR fog. 

Increasing off surface winds add extra difficulty to temporal 

and spatial extent of any impacts. Limited winds through central

Iowa at 08.00Z TAF issuance influenced addition of MVFR-IFR impacts

to TAF sites. Have introduced TEMPO IFR at KLSE with susceptibility

to lower winds. 



Any impacts will lift through the morning hours. Subsequent 

impacts accompany increasing storm chances overnight Friday 

through Saturday morning. Line of strong to severe storms 

possible Saturday will provide transient LIFR/IFR impacts where 

storms form. Storms linger through the weekend into Monday 

morning primarily impacting southern half of the forecast area 

from northeast Iowa into southwest Wisconsin. Northern extent 

may impact local TAF sites (KLSE/KRST).



&&



.ARX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

WI...None.

MN...None.

IA...None.



&&



$$



DISCUSSION...Naylor

AVIATION...JAR



