403

FXUS65 KCYS 080000

AFDCYS



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Cheyenne WY

600 PM MDT Thu Aug 7 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Largely hot and dry conditions are expected across the area

  through Friday. Areas of smoke from wildfires to our southwest

  are expected to produce periods of degraded air quality.



- An isolated strong to severe thunderstorm cannot be ruled out

  this afternoon/evening, mainly in the northern NE panhandle 

  and east central WY.



- Red Flag Warnings remain in effect through Friday due to low 

  afternoon relative humidity and poor overnight humidity 

  recoveries.



&&



.SHORT TERM /THROUGH SATURDAY/...

Issued at 231 PM MDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Warm and dry today but tomorrow we should begin to see the

return of moisture across the region which will return us back

to a chance for severe weather.



Ongoing red flag warnings today will continue into tomorrow, 

and some locations may even need to see that extended into 

Saturday as RH values plummet once again before we see a very 

modest at best recovery thereafter. The other note to today's 

weather was the cloud cover which helped to limit some of our 

high temperatures across the region, with Cheyenne struggling to

near 90 degrees thanks to this ceiling. With today expected to 

be the warmest day of the period, this was a nice respite for 

our area, though of course the Panhandle was not so lucky as 

triple digits abound were present. Otherwise the rest of this 

evening's forecast looks to remain quiet and on track, with 

almost all activity expected just north and east of our Nebraska

Panhandle zones, and no significant activity anticipated.



Tomorrow a cold front pushes through the region and should bring

some relief to the heat as temperatures descend back into the

70's to low 90's. While this will also help with RH values and

could bring some relief to fire weather concerns, a lack of

surface moisture is still going to fuel some concerns at least

for our western zones in Carbon and Albany Counties. We are

seeing values starting to show back into the the upper teens to

very low 20's, so some of these warnings may be allowed to

expire early. With the return of some cooler temperatures and

moisture aloft though, thunderstorm chances also return to the

area as well. Most of the favorable instability for stronger

weather once again lies further west of our region, but some

MUCAPE values of 500-1000 J/kg are showing up on the HREF for

our Panhandle zones as well as our far northeastern counties of

Wyoming (Converse, Niobrara, and Goshen), with a marginal risk

for hail and winds present. High res guidance does present a

couple of stronger cells for both Wyoming and western Nebraska

before the evening is up, so look for some thunder with a

potential for a stronger to severe possible. 



&&



.LONG TERM /SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...

Issued at 231 PM MDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Activity will creep up again into the weekend as an upper level low 

passes over the northern Rockies and northern Plains. Expect cooler 

temperatures on Saturday with highs generally in the upper 70s to 

mid 80s underneath the slow moving trough aloft. A dry slot of the 

upper level low is expected to creep into much of the northern part 

of the forecast area, and mix down very low dewpoints in the 

afternoon hours. Still, most guidance is holding onto a more moist 

surface environment along and southeast of roughly a Cheyenne to 

Alliance line. Convergence along the dryline may provide enough 

forcing to kick off some afternoon and evening showers and 

thunderstorms. While instability doesn't look too impressive, there 

will be plenty of shear present and fairly good deep moisture, at 

least on the wet side of the dryline. A few scattered strong to 

severe thunderstorms and locally heavy rainfall could be possible 

with Saturday's activity. There is also a chance for forcing to 

continue late into the overnight hours or even Sunday morning. As a 

result, PoPs are retained for far southeast WY and the southern NE 

panhandle into Sunday morning. 



Expect temperatures to reach their minimum on Sunday with 700-mb 

temperatures around +7 to +9C, which is actually close to the 

climatological 10th percentile. Cloud cover over the southern part 

of the forecast area may keep much of the I-80 corridor in the mid 

70s or cooler on Sunday while the drier northern zones will still be 

cooler than average, but should still warm to the low to mid 80s. 

Look for another round of widely scattered showers and 

thunderstorms, mainly along/south of the North Platte River Valley. 

Current soundings appear to be more garden-variety thunderstorms 

with some locally heavy rainfall threat, but there is time for this 

to change. CIN may be an issue for both Saturday and Sunday's 

convection.



Expect to see a warming and drying trend through the first half of 

next week as ridging tried to build back in over the western and 

central CONUS. 



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z Saturday/...

Issued at 600 PM MDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Strengthening southwest flow aloft is expected tonight and Friday

as a pretty strong upper level trough and associated cold front 

moves south and east across the northern Rockys and into the central 

and northern plains. Winds are forecast to diminish gradually this

evening with winds shifting into the northwest on Friday. 



HAZARDS/WEATHER TRENDS: VFR conditions will prevail across the

area tonight and into Friday morning with intervals of midlevel 

cloudiness. Any smoke or haze from area wildfires is not expected to 

result in lower surface VIS at this time. 





&&



.CYS WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

WY...Red Flag Warning until 8 PM MDT this evening for WYZ417-418.

     Red Flag Warning until 6 PM MDT Friday for WYZ419>429.

NE...Red Flag Warning until 8 PM MDT this evening for NEZ434.



&&



