=================================================



869

FXUS62 KRAH 080015

AFDRAH



Area Forecast Discussion 

National Weather Service Raleigh NC 

815 PM EDT Thu Aug 7 2025



.SYNOPSIS...

A mid and upper-level low will pivot slowly east and across the 

region through early Friday. Canadian surface high pressure will 

remain in place down the eastern seaboard through the weekend, then 

shift offshore by Monday. 



&&



.NEAR TERM /THIS AFTERNOON THROUGH TONIGHT/...

As of 1225 PM Thursday...



* Considerable cloudiness, areas of light rain/drizzle/fog, and 

  unseasonably cool



Satellite and radar data indicate considerable cloudiness across 

central NC at early afternoon. There continued to be areas of 

light rain, drizzle, and fog over the region. The surface high 

pressure (~1030 mb) was centered over the NE states extending down 

the eastern seaboard. This pattern, along with the nearly 

persistent mid/upper trough over the eastern US (trough axis over 

the Appalachians), continued to bring a SW flow aloft with 

occasional perturbations overtop the NE surface flow that 

continued to result in the rain and CAD event. 



The mid/upper trough is expected to slowly weaken and drift east 

over the area late tonight. Stable conditions with the ongoing CAD 

will likely continue through tonight. This will mean areas of light 

rain, drizzle, and fog will continue this afternoon. Highs will hold 

in the lower to mid 70s (continued well below normal for early 

August by 15-20 degrees due to the CAD). Most of the needed lift for 

organized areas of rain will weaken tonight. This will leave 

considerable cloudiness with areas of drizzle. Some patchy light 

rain will continue, mainly over the eastern Piedmont into the 

Coastal Plain. Lows generally in the mid to upper 60s.



&&



.SHORT TERM /FRIDAY AND FRIDAY NIGHT/...

As of 1245 PM Thursday... 



* Isolated to scattered showers/storms, best coverage over Sandhills 

  and srn/cntl Coastal Plain, during the afternoon and evening.



* Below normal temperatures continue.



Aloft, the weakening mid-level trough will shift slowly enewd out of 

the area through Fri night. The potent upper level s/w will track 

slowly ewd then newd across the area through Fri evening. Farther 

west, the mid-level anti-cyclone will remain over the Desert 

Southwest/nrn Mexico, ridging enewd across the Plains and into the 

mid-MS Valley, as a deepening closed low tracks ewd along the west-

central US/Canada border. At the surface, high pressure will 

continue ridging swwd across the mid-Atlantic, while the coastal low 

gradually deepens and lifts newd off the Carolina coast. Continued 

cool nely flow will prevail at the surface. The wedge front will 

again be draped ssw-nne across ern portions of the area on Fri, 

separating the cool stable air NW from the more warm, moist, 

unstable air east.



Precipitation: PWATs could range from around 1.5 inches in the NW to 

1.8-2.0 inches over the Sandhills and Coastal Plain Fri aft/eve. NAM 

forecast soundings show SBCAPE ranging from 300 J/Kg NW to 800 J/Kg 

east, while the 15Z RAP has no SBCAPE in the NW, with a max around 

500 J/Kg at KFAY and less than 200 J/Kg at KRDU and KRWI. With the 

potent s/w moving over the Sandhills and Coastal Plain, portions of 

those regions along and on the warm side of the wedge front could 

see isolated to scattered showers and storms, with some additional 

heavy rainfall possible where they occur. However, the exact 

location of the wedge front remains uncertain. 



For temperatures, continued below normal highs (by 6-12 degrees), 

ranging from mid/upper 70s north to low 80s south and east. Lows 

should be within a category of normal, mainly in the 60s. 



&&



.LONG TERM /SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...

As of 110 PM Thursday... 



*  A relatively dry period this weekend, although a stray shower 

   cannot be ruled out. Then, diurnally driven showers and storms 

   possible each day through mid-week.



*  Gradual moderation in temperatures through mid-week. 



Aloft, As the upper level trough shifts ewd out of the area on Sat, 

the sub-tropical ridge will build nwd over the region and remain in 

place through mid-week. An upper low should close off and rapidly 

deepen while lingering off the mid-Atlantic/Northeast US coast Sat-

Tue before drifting ewd over the North Atlantic. At the surface, 

high pressure will continue ridging swwd into the area over the 

weekend as the coastal low deepens and lifts slowly enewd over the 

NW Atlantic. The evolution of the low and ridge thereafter remains 

uncertain, varying between the available guidance. Generally, the 

low should gradually move away from the East Coast, allowing the 

high to slide sewd across the mid-Atlantic and offshore, while still 

potentially ridging wwd into central NC through mid-week. Expect 

relatively dry weather across much of the area over the weekend, 

although cannot rule out some showers during the aft/eve across the 

south and east. Early to mid next week, will continue with the 

climatological, diurnal chances for showers/storms each day. For 

temperatures, highs will gradually moderate from below normal on Sat 

to slightly above normal by Wed, while lows will also moderate from 

near normal to slightly above normal.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...

As of 815 PM Thursday...



Areas of light rain and drizzle will persist this evening, 

especially at KINT and KGSO. Any improvement to VFR conditions will 

be short-lived as widespread IFR ceilings are expected to re-develop 

between 06 to 12z. 



Gradual improvement is expected during the day Friday, with ceilings 

lifting to MVFR between 15-18z, and then improving to VFR during the 

afternoon. However, KRWI may remain under MVFR ceilings through the 

TAF period. 



Outlook: Continued nely flow will persist through at least Sat, 

during which time flight restrictions mostly from ceilings will be 

lowest each night-morning, with diurnal improvement to MVFR or low 

VFR each afternoon, along with a small chance of showers. The 

nely flow regime will gradually break down and transition to a more-

typical, summertime pattern of mostly VFR conditions away from 

diurnal convection by early next week. 



&&



.RAH WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

None. 



&&



$$



SYNOPSIS...Badgett/MWS

NEAR TERM...Badgett

SHORT TERM...KC

LONG TERM...KC

AVIATION...CBL/MWS



