[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.572","issue":"2025-08-08T00:20:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS65","cccc":"KBOU","awipsid":"AFDBOU"}]
=================================================



994

FXUS65 KBOU 080020

AFDBOU



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Denver/Boulder CO

620 PM MDT Thu Aug 7 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...  

 

- Critical fire weather conditions become widespread across the 

  mountains, high valleys, and foothills Friday.



- Hot and dry conditions persist into Friday with highs in the 

  mid to upper 90s.



- Some relief from the heat this weekend with a chance of storms 

  Saturday and Sunday. 



&&



.DISCUSSION /Through Thursday/...

Issued at 220 PM MDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Southwest flow around the strong upper level high over the 

Southern Rockies continues tonight and into Friday. This has led 

to a hot day with most locations across the urban corridor in the 

mid 90s by noon. Satellite imagery shows an increase in mid/upper 

level moisture, this should produce isolated high-based showers 

through early evening. Little to no rain is expected with the 

showers, but wind gusts to 50 mph will be possible. Flow aloft 

increases during the day Friday as an upper level trough passes to

our north. The increase in winds and slightly drier air will 

result in widespread critical fire weather conditions across the 

higher terrain for Friday (see Fire Weather section for details). 

Cross sections show, slight drying for Friday, so we are not 

expecting much if any high- based shower activity. Highs will be a

few degrees cooler as the upper level high is nudged slightly to 

the south. However, temperatures are still expected to reach the 

mid to upper 90s over northeast Colorado. 



A cold front associated with the upper level trough to the north 

pushes through Colorado Friday evening. A couple models are 

starting to show storms developing behind the front around and 

after midnight. A trend that needs to be watched. The cold front 

will usher in somewhat cooler air for Saturday with highs in the 

mid to upper 80s. Moisture also increases for Saturday leading to 

scattered afternoon and evening showers and thunderstorms. The 

mountains and high valleys only see a glancing shot of cooler and 

increased moisture. On Saturday, temperatures are expected to be 2

to 5 degrees cooler over the mountains while on the plains, 

temperatures should be about 10 degrees cooler than Friday. Where 

moisture is deep enough, MLCAPE of 1000-2000 J/kg is expected 

Saturday and Sunday across the plains, and possibly along the 

urban corridor. Shear will be good enough on Saturday, and 

possibly Sunday to support severe thunderstorms. With this still 

2-3 days away, much uncertainty remains regarding the severe 

threat with the amount of moisture (may end up with not enough for

severe storms) and a possible cap in place. Better chance for 

storms appears to be Sunday as an upper level trough moves through

the region.



For next week, a drying and warming trend is expected. Monday's 

highs may stay below 90F across northeast Colorado. Subsidence and

drying behind the exiting trough is expected to result in mostly 

dry conditions. The ridge begins to rebuild over the region 

Tuesday with highs expected to climb above 90 over northeast 

Colorado. The warm temperatures are expected to continue for 

Wednesday and Thursday as the upper level high moves across the 

region. Precipitation chances look to be low with limited moisture

expected.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS through 00Z Saturday/...

Issued at 607 PM MDT Thu Aug 7 2025



VFR through Friday. Some variability in the winds is expected

through 02-03z, mostly with speeds under 10 knots, but then 

normal south/west drainage winds will kick in. On Friday, westerly

winds will develop 15z-18z with gusts to 25 knots in the

afternoon.



&&



.FIRE WEATHER...

Issued at 1107 AM MDT Thu Aug 7 2025



A hot and mostly dry southwest flow aloft around the upper level 

high to the south will persist through Friday. Water vapor 

satellite imagery shows a little mid level moisture passing 

through the area, which is helping to keep the relative humidities

in the mountains above 15 percent. This slight increase in 

moisture should produce isolated high-based showers. Gusty winds 

to 50 mph will be possible, while little to no rain is expected 

from the high-based showers. 



For Friday, cross sections show the mid levels dry some. A strong

upper level trough travels along the U.S./Canada border. This 

will increase flow aloft and is expected to bringing windier 

conditions (gust to 40 mph) for Friday. Temperatures decrease a 

few degrees, but the drier air will keep relative humidities low. 

Upgraded the Fire Weather Watch to a Red Flag Warning for the 

mountains, high valleys, and foothills earlier today.



&&



.BOU WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

Red Flag Warning until 8 PM MDT this evening for COZ211-213-214-

217.



Red Flag Warning from 10 AM to 8 PM MDT Friday for COZ211>218.



Heat Advisory until 7 PM MDT this evening for COZ038>040.



&&



$$



DISCUSSION...Meier

AVIATION...Gimmestad

FIRE WEATHER...Meier



