=================================================



237

FXAK68 PAFC 080028

AFDAFC



Southcentral and Southwest Alaska Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Anchorage AK

428 PM AKDT Thu Aug 7 2025



.SHORT TERM FORECAST SOUTHCENTRAL ALASKA (Days 1 through 3)...



Discussion:



Rain that overspread Anchorage and the Kenai Peninsula this 

morning and early afternoon has begun to become more scattered and

showery in nature while persistent rainfall continues along the 

Gulf coast. Steadier precipitation continues for the Copper River 

Basin and Susitna Valley through this evening as the front reaches

the coast and begins to weaken. The timing of additional rainfall

across the western Kenai Peninsula up through the Mat Valley is 

the main forecast challenge, with additional showers likely to 

develop this evening into Friday morning as upper level waves 

continue to track across the region and southeasterly cross- 

barrier flow and downsloping remains rather modest. Surface and 

buoy observations in the northern Gulf continue to show easterly 

gale- force gusts while winds remain elevated through the typical 

east/west gap zones across Southcentral. These winds will 

gradually decrease overnight as the front lifts inland and stalls.



Coastal rainfall and generally unsettled conditions across 

Southcentral continue through the weekend as the upper low lingers

over the AKPen before lifting north into the interior as an open 

wave. Several more upper-level disturbances are set to follow the 

departure of the low as a quick-moving shortwave lifts out of the 

North Pacific and a trough dives southward across western Alaska 

and into Southcentral by the end of the weekend. 



-JH



&&





.SHORT TERM FORECAST SOUTHWEST ALASKA/BERING SEA/ALEUTIANS 

(Days 1 through 3: Today through Saturday night)...



A low pressure system in the southeastern Bering Sea and the 

remnants of a trough continue ongoing rainfall across mainland 

Southwest Alaska. Northerly flow across the Bering in the wake of 

the low is maintaining the presence of widespread stratus and 

areas of fog in the central Bering Sea. Substantial cloud cover 

continues to keep conditions in Southwest Alaska cool and wet, 

decreasing daytime high temperatures. Southeasterly winds 

associated with the occluded front over Southwest to diminish 

steadily tonight. The low opens up into a trough tonight south of 

the Alaska Peninsula and a brief dry period is expected Friday 

morning, with only isolated shower activity lingering in the Lower

Kuskokwim Valley and Delta. 



By Friday afternoon, a new upper-level low pressure system drops 

over the northern Bering Sea and Kuskokwim Delta, re-initiating 

rainfall across the Kuskokwim Delta. This is expected to form a 

trough axis establishing from near Crooked Creek to near Bethel. 

This compact front will bring a period of mostly light rainfall to

the area, and begin sweeping slowly to the east through Friday 

night, orienting over Lime Village to Iliamna by Saturday 

afternoon. Coastal Bristol Bay may remain relatively dry even as 

cold air sweeps as far south as the eastern Aleutians and Alaska 

Peninsula this weekend. Expected a steady, but not strong 

northwesterly flow over much of Southwest Alaska with frontal 

push. Over the Bering, widespread stratus clouds to persist, with 

limited areas of fog, and generally dry conditions. Until Friday 

evening as a new low pressure system, over the Kamchatka Peninsula is

set to push a front into the western Aleutians, bringing a new 

source of rain to the Aleutians. Through Saturday and Saturday 

evening the new western Bering front will steadily spread 

eastward, reaching Amchitka by Sunday morning. 



-CL



&&





.LONG TERM FORECAST (Days 4 through 7: Monday through 

Thursday)...



An active weather pattern is setting up across the state for the 

long term forecast period. Rain across Southcentral will likely 

dissipate by early Monday as the low progresses quickly to the 

southeast. Upper- level ridging then amplifies across Southcentral

in the wake of the trough, while a compact shortwave rides under 

the ridge increasing potential for rain Tuesday. There is 

uncertainty on how far inland rain will make it with the ECMWF 

brining it over most of Southcentral while the GFS keeps the 

precipitation mostly coastal. The Bering remains active as a 

series of troughs rotate across the Aleutians and into mainland 

Alaska. Model agreement deteriorates greatly by this time, but 

continued active weather is likely for all of southern Alaska. 

After a cool weekend, temperatures across the region will warm to 

or above average Tuesday and Wednesday. 



&&



.AVIATION...



PANC...Rain showers will continue through Friday morning with 

ceilings generally remaining in VFR and periods of visibility to 

MVFR.



&&





$$



