Riverton Aviation Discussion

Issued at 2319 UTC Thursday, August  7, 2025



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z SATURDAY/...

Issued at 515 PM MDT Thu Aug 7 2025

Hot, dry and windy continues across western and central WY late

this afternoon and into the early evening hours. VFR conditions

are expected at all sites through the forecast period, though 

there is a small chance of MVFR conditions due to lower ceilings 

at KCOD/KWRL/KCPR as a cold front brings moisture from the 

north. The gusty winds will decrease by sunset, though a few 

sites may remain breezy overnight. WSW winds have struggled to 

get into KCOD though, so likely going persistence with light NE 

wind. Have added some VCSH to KJAC/KCOD/KWRL for varying times 

overnight due to a 20% chance of light showers with the initial 

mid-level push of the cold front from the northwest. Any heavier

showers and isolated thunderstorms are expected to remain 

across the WY/MT border, then into the northern Bighorn 

Mountains after midnight. The cold front is first expected to 

impact KCOD around 08/03Z with gusty north winds, then move 

south to KWRL. KCPR will also see the wind shift just after 

08/06Z as the front moves from Johnson into Natrona County. As 

mentioned, these areas should see some lower ceilings, though 

uncertain with the height.

As the initial wave moves southeast Friday morning, mid-level clouds 

are expected to drape across the state from SW WY toward east-

central WY with lingering lower(ish) clouds east of the Continental

Divide. Post-frontal WNW winds will increase by 08/18Z across 

western and southern WY and push off the Absarokas and Wind 

Rivers during the afternoon to create a boundary east of the 

Divide, where winds on the NE side will be from the north. 

Gusts are expected to be 25-30 kt at times. Weaker northerly 

flow will then develop late Friday evening. Some showers and 

very isolated thunderstorms may develop over the Bighorns Friday

afternoon and move eastward. Have added VCSH to KCOD and KWRL 

for a 25% chance of light showers, and given KCPR a 25-30% 

chance of showers and thunderstorms from 08/21-09/00Z.

Please see the Aviation Weather Center and/or CWSU ZDV and ZLC for 

the latest information on smoke, icing, and turbulence

forecasts.



Cheyenne Aviation Discussion

Issued at 0000 UTC Friday, August  8, 2025



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z Saturday/...

Issued at 600 PM MDT Thu Aug 7 2025

Strengthening southwest flow aloft is expected tonight and Friday

as a pretty strong upper level trough and associated cold front 

moves south and east across the northern Rockys and into the central 

and northern plains. Winds are forecast to diminish gradually this

evening with winds shifting into the northwest on Friday. 

HAZARDS/WEATHER TRENDS: VFR conditions will prevail across the

area tonight and into Friday morning with intervals of midlevel 

cloudiness. Any smoke or haze from area wildfires is not expected to 

result in lower surface VIS at this time. 





