Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Nashville TN

729 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



...New UPDATE...



.KEY MESSAGES...

Updated at 722 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



- Isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms this afternoon.

  Severe thunderstorm chances are very low and not expected.

  Greatest coverage of precipitation will be near the plateau. 



- Temperatures will be at or just above seasonal normals through

  the rest of this week, and a little warmer next week. Humidity

  will be tolerable but creeping upward toward the end of the

  7-day forecast period.



- There are very low chances for rain and thunderstorms through

  the weekend, but rain chances will increase somewhat starting on

  Monday. The severe storm threat is very low for the foreseeable

  future.



&&



.UPDATE...

Issued at 722 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



We did end up with a bit more convective activity during the

afternoon than previously expected, but it has almost entirely

dissipated now that sunset is upon us. The evening sounding from 

OHX shows that despite the appearance of an elevated inversion in

the 500-550 mb layer, today's air mass is exhibiting somewhat 

greater instability than 24 hrs. ago, and also slightly higher 

PWAT values. Overnight, we expect somewhat less radiation 

fog...mainly just along river valleys and low-lying areas. 



&&



.SHORT TERM...

(Rest of today through Friday)

Issued at 1143 AM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



The Midstate remains in weak N/NW flow aloft with an upper ridge

across the Great Plains. A weak MCV moving southeast will help

initiate more scattered convection this afternoon with the

greatest coverage near and south of Interstate 40, and along the

Cumberland Plateau. RAP forecast soundings indicate meager

instability around 1000 J/Kg ahead of this MCV with unimpressive

PW values and weak EBShear around 10 kt. Warm mid-level 

temperatures will limit overall instability and convective 

severity. For this reason, general isolated to scattered rain 

showers, and a few ordinary pulse thunderstorms, are all that is 

expected this afternoon and evening. 



&&



.LONG TERM...

(Friday Night through next Thursday)

Issued at 1143 AM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



The prevailing pattern continues into the weekend with similar

airmass characteristics and diurnal convective chances (mainly 

across the plateau). With the continued NW flow aloft, there will

be limited chances for remnant MCVs to locally enhance convective

chances, but there is currently no signal for widespread

precipitation chances through the weekend. Next week, surface high

pressure shifts eastward with a return of southern, moisture-rich

air which will increase dew points across the region. Upper-level

ridging is expected to increase across the Tennessee Valley with

warm temperatures pushing heat index values near 100F.



&&



.AVIATION...

(00Z TAFS)

Issued at 602 PM CDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Shower and thunderstorm coverage will diminish shortly after 

sunset. Patchy fog develops overnight with highlight confidence in

MVFR visibilities at CSV and SRB. Fog will diminish around 14Z 

with VFR prevailing thereafter. Chance for thunderstorm impacts at

any terminals Friday afternoon is very low (15% or less).



&&



