=================================================



643

AXPQ20 PGUM 080043

TWDPQ 



Tropical Weather Discussion

National Weather Service Tiyan GU

1043 AM ChST Fri Aug 8 2025



Tropical Weather Discussion for the Western North Pacific between

the Equator and 25N from 130E to 180. The following information is

based on recent satellite imagery/data, weather observations, radar,

and meteorological analysis.



...SPECIAL FEATURES...



TROPICAL CYCLONES...

One tropical cyclone is found in the region. At 7:00 AM, Tropical

Storm Podul (16W) was centered near 19.7N 145.9E, moving northwest

at 10 mph. Maximum sustained winds were 40 mph. Convection 

associated with Podul is asymmetrical, with only isolated to low-end

scattered showers seen north of the storm and numerous to widespread

showers and isolated thunderstorms found south of the center, 

extending as far as 185 miles south. This convection includes 

northern CNMI islands south of Maug to north of Sarigan. 16W is 

expected to turn more toward the west through Saturday with no 

change in forward speed. 16W is expected to continue to slowly 

strengthen over the next few days.



For more information please refer to bulletins from the Joint

Typhoon Warning Center under WMO header WTPN32 PGTW and from the

National Weather Service under WMO header WTPQ32 PGUM and WTPQ82 

PGUM.



...OTHER WEATHER SYSTEMS...



MONSOON TROUGH...

A weak monsoon trough extends eastward from the Philippines to

cross 130E near 15N, turning east-northeast to end at TS Podul.

Only spotty showers are found along the trough except near 130E,

where numerous to widespread showers and isolated thunderstorms

are seen west of 131E. The weak trough looks to weaken further as

it drifts north due to the movement of 16W and a high pressure 

ridge strengthening across the Philippine Sea.



TRADE-WIND TROUGHS...

There are several trade-wind troughs across the region. The first

of these is found well south of Guam between Eauripik and Ifalik,

extending north-northeast from near 2N142E to near 10N144E. 

Scattered to numerous showers and isolated thunderstorms are found

along and west of the trough. The trough will continue to slowly 

drift west over the coming days with little change in intensity, 

eventually bringing increased convection to western Yap State, 

including Yap Proper.



The next trough is found east of Weno, Chuuk, extending northeast

from near 4N151E to end near 10N160E. Scattered to numerous

showers and isolated thunderstorms are seen across Chuuk State,

including Weno. The trough will continue to progress west over 

the next few days with little change in intensity.



The next trough is seen between Pohnpei and Kosrae to Kwajalein,

extending east-northeast from near 4N159E to near 9N169E.

Scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms are found along the

trough. This trough looks to continue moving west over the next

few days as it strengthens, bringing increased convection into

Pohnpei.



Another trough is found north of Kwajalein, extending north from

near 10N168E to 16N167E. This trough is interacting with

divergence associated with the TUTT to produce numerous showers

and isolated thunderstorms over the north Marshall Islands between

10N and 16N. The trough will push west over the coming days with 

little change in intensity.



TUTT...

The TUTT enters the region near 25N180, extending southwest, passing

through a TUTT cell centered near 24N178E to another cell centered

near 17N162E. The TUTT then continues southwest to end near

another circulation centered near 3N147E. Upper-level divergence

associated with the three circulations is generating scattered to

numerous showers and isolated thunderstorms southwest of Pearl and

Hermes Atoll, north of Kwajalein and over Chuuk. The TUTT and

cells are expected to remain quasi-stationary with little change

in intensity over the next few days.



OTHER SYSTEMS...

A weak high pressure surface ridge is found over western Yap State

and Palau, promoting fair weather across most of Palau and eastern

Yap State, with scattered showers seen along the western periphery

where weak convergence is occurring. This is just west of Koror,

Palau. This ridge looks to move northwest and strengthen over the

next couple of days, spreading drier conditions across all of 

Palau, western Yap State and the Philippine Sea.



$$



Kleeschulte



