2025-08-08T01-02-20-117Z

[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.1713","issue":"2025-08-08T01:02:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS62","cccc":"KTAE","awipsid":"AFDTAE"}]
=================================================



148

FXUS62 KTAE 080102

AFDTAE



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Tallahassee FL

902 PM EDT Thu Aug 7 2025



...New UPDATE...



.UPDATE...

Issued at 900 PM EDT Thu Aug 7 2025



The forecast appears to be on track, minimal updates appear

necessary.



&&



.NEAR TERM...

(Through Friday)

Issued at 235 PM EDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Drier conditions across Alabama and Georgia will keep rain chances 

lower this afternoon but moist conditions across Florida will keep 

rain chances high. Northeasterly flow at the surface has kept 

conditions fairly stable across much of the region with stratus 

prevailing, but the stratus is beginning to break up with showers 

and storms developing where stratus has thinned out and a more 

robust cumulus field has developed. Main concerns today is with 

heavy rain. Widespread flooding rains aren't anticipated but slow 

movers are forecast and isolated 3 to 4 inch rainfall amounts can't 

be ruled out. 



Broad upper level trough and low-level northeasterly flow should be 

continuing tomorrow but deep-layer moisture should be a little 

higher across the area so rain chances should be a little higher. 

Slow storm motions again are possible which could lead to localized 

areas of flooding in low-lying areas and poor drainage areas. With 

the cooler northeasterly flow subsiding somewhat tomorrow and a 

little less stratus in the morning, we should be able to warm up a 

few degrees higher than this afternoon.



&&



.SHORT TERM AND LONG TERM...

(Friday night through Wednesday)

Issued at 235 PM EDT Thu Aug 7 2025



A tropical wave will slide into the northeast Gulf this weekend 

while a meandering front will slowly sag south into the Gulf waters 

before dissipating Monday. PWATs bump back up over 2.1 inches most 

locations which will lead to an increase in rain chances areawide. 

Scattered to widespread showers and thunderstorms will occur each 

day through Monday, although convection will be more diurnally 

driven and more typical for summertime. Gusty winds and locally 

heavy rainfall will accompany stronger convection. Northeasterly 

winds for much of the week will also tend to favor convection over 

our FL counties as the ambient flow and seabreeze collide.



Beyond Monday, the subtropical ridge gains influence into the 

southeast US with rain chances declining back to more typical levels 

for August. In conjunction, highs will jump back into the low to mid 

90s and heat indices pushing back towards 108F possibly requiring 

heat advisories for the middle of next week.



&&



.AVIATION...

(00Z TAFS)

Issued at 708 PM EDT Thu Aug 7 2025



VFR cigs are expected to prevail through the rest of the evening.

Low cigs will be possible around daybreak in the morning for ABY 

and VLD terminals with MVFR and possibly IFR ceilings. Isolated to

scattered showers/thunderstorms will be possible later in the 

afternoon for TLH and VLD terminals. ECP may see a shower or two 

but confidence is too low to include in the TAFs at this time. 

Winds will be light from the northeast. 



&&



.MARINE...

Issued at 235 PM EDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Favorable marine conditions will persist into the weekend. A 

tropical wave will enter the northern Gulf Saturday through Monday

with an increase in showers and thunderstorms each day favoring 

the overnight into morning periods. Beyond Monday, high pressure 

builds through the southeast US with winds becoming east to 

southeast and an overall decreasing trend to the rain chances.



&&



.FIRE WEATHER...

Issued at 235 PM EDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Low fire weather concerns are forecast the next few days with

moist conditions and increasing rain chances expected into the

weekend. The wettest locations will likely be the Florida Big

Bend, with lower rain chances across southeast Alabama. Only

concerns will be dispersions on the lower side, but most

locations should still remain above 25 into the weekend.



&&



.HYDROLOGY...

Issued at 235 PM EDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Scattered to widespread showers and storms are expected each day

through the weekend and into early next week, with the greatest

coverage generally expected over the FL counties. Widespread

flooding is not expected, although locally heavy rainfall could

cause isolated flooding issues in urban or already saturated

areas. 



Several rivers and streams remain in or are forecast to reach

action stage due to recent rainfall. However, no flooding is

forecast at this time. 



&&



.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...



Spotter activation is not requested. However, spotters are always 

encouraged to safely report significant weather conditions when they 

occur by calling the office or tweeting us @NWSTallahassee.



&&



