[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.2157","issue":"2025-08-08T01:16:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS62","cccc":"KFFC","awipsid":"AFDFFC"}]
=================================================



593

FXUS62 KFFC 080116

AFDFFC



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Peachtree City GA

916 PM EDT Thu Aug 7 2025



...Evening Update...



.UPDATE...

Issued at 914 PM EDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Thunderstorms in north Georgia continue to taper off as

instability wanes with sunset. Some rainfall remains likely,

however lightning activity has fallen to near zero. Overnight

hours may see a return to cloudy conditions, likely a repeat/near

repeat of this morning.



&&



.SHORT TERM...

(This afternoon through Friday)

Issued at 228 PM EDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Key Messages:



- Pleasant temperatures and cloudy skies.



- Some scattered showers and thunderstorms; mainly in the northern 

and southern parts of the CWA.



Forecast:



Wedge conditions are still in place, however it's not nearly as 

strong as it has been in the past few days. What does that mean? 

Well temperatures will be cooler than what we'd normally expect for 

early August with temperatures in the mid to low 80s today and 

tomorrow. Overnight lows are expected to be in the mid to upper 60s. 

Overall the vibe is more in line with October than August. If you're 

excited for Pumpkin drinks and spooky decor, these next few days 

should get you in the mood.



Rain chances will be limited to northern Georgia, as NE flow will 

force what little moisture in the atmosphere to interact with some 

of the higher points in NE Georgia. Showers and thunderstorms will 

be scattered by nature and confined to the GA/TN border region. 

Across the southern part of the CWA, the sea breeze may cause a few 

storms to pop up at the far southern end of our CWA as it interacts 

with the winds from the wedge combined with the stalled front across 

Florida. 



Overall though, it should be a very nice day across Georgia. So get 

out there and enjoy it!



Vaughn



&&



.LONG TERM...

(Saturday morning through next Wednesday)

Issued at 228 PM EDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Key Messages:



  - Mild weather continues through the weekend as NE flow remains 

over north and central Georgia. Expect highs in the 80s and lows in 

the upper 60s and lower 70s, close to seasonal averages.



  - August finally decides to show up into the middle of next

    week. High temperatures will return to the upper 80s to lower

    90s with diurnally driven storm chances.



Forecast:



Surface high pressure over the northeast in combination with the 

potential development of a surface low off the Delmarva will lock in 

continued northeasterly flow over the CWA through the weekend 

into the beginning of next week. Airmass is no longer directly 

tied to Canadian continental air that brought the very cool air 

earlier this week, but instead will be a bit more modified with 

Atlantic moisture and be a bit warmer, allowing for some increase 

in diurnally driven convection across the area (provided we can 

clear out low morning cloud cover on these days, which can be a 

separate issue). Temperatures through this weekend should remain 

in the 80s across north and central Georgia, while afternoon rain 

chances slowly increase into early next week.



Reason we continue to see cooler air will be a combo of ridging 

aloft with confluence over the northeast keeping a surface high 

hanging about, combined with the formation of a cut-off low over the 

Atlantic that looks to generate a surface low off the Delmarva coast 

as mentioned above. This will keep a psuedo-wedge setup in place 

while still pumping in some Atlantic surface moisture. This setup 

looks to change going into the middle of next week, with model 

ensembles consistent in showing a strong trough digging into the 

midwest with attendant surface low that should finally help turn 

our winds a bit to the south at the surface and allow real Georgia

in August weather to come back into the area. Enjoy the reprieve 

from the heat while it lasts, especially this time of year.



Lusk



&&



.AVIATION...

(00Z TAFS)

Issued at 726 PM EDT Thu Aug 7 2025



Conditions have improved to VFR across the region with some low sct 

VFR at northern sites from showers. No precip impacts expected at 

any taf sites through period. TEMPO for another morning of low CIGS 

with possibility of LIFR conditions tomorrow morning. Winds remain 

out of ENE at less than 10kts.



//ATL Confidence...00Z Update...

High confidence all elements.



SM



&&



